/* curriculum-data.js — IV/2SLS, DiD, VAR, Panel, Logit, GMM.
   Authored + verified by the econ-curriculum workflow. Extends window.CURRICULUM. */
Object.assign(window.CURRICULUM, {"iv2sls":{"id":"iv2sls","title":"Instrumental Variables & 2SLS","modules":[{"id":"iv2sls-why-ols-fails","title":"1 · Why OLS fails","summary":"Endogeneity from omitted variables and simultaneity makes OLS biased and inconsistent.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"The exogeneity assumption","html":"<h2>What OLS quietly assumes</h2><p>Write the model as</p><p class=\"katexish\">y = β₀ + β₁·x + ε</p><p>OLS gives a consistent, unbiased estimate of β₁ <strong>only if the regressor is exogenous</strong>: the error term ε must be uncorrelated with x, written <code>Cov(x, ε) = 0</code>. Intuitively, everything we left out of the model (bundled into ε) must not move together with x.</p><p>When instead <code>Cov(x, ε) ≠ 0</code> we say x is <em>endogenous</em>. OLS then attributes to x variation that really belongs to ε, and β̂₁ is pulled away from the truth.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> OLS regresses y on <em>all</em> the comovement between x and y. If part of that comovement runs through ε, OLS cannot tell the causal part from the contaminated part — and the bias does <em>not</em> shrink as n → ∞.</div><h3>Two classic ways endogeneity arises</h3><ul><li><strong>Omitted variables.</strong> A confounder u drives both x and y. Leaving u out dumps it into ε, so x and ε share u.</li><li><strong>Simultaneity / reverse causality.</strong> y also feeds back into x (price ↔ quantity, police ↔ crime), so x responds to the same shocks as y.</li></ul>"},{"type":"read","title":"The sign and size of the bias","html":"<h2>How big is the OLS bias?</h2><p>Suppose the true structural model has a confounder u that we cannot observe:</p><p class=\"katexish\">x = π·u + v,  y = β₀ + β₁·x + γ·u + η</p><p>Because u appears in both equations, x carries information about u, and u sits inside the regression error ε = γ·u + η. The asymptotic OLS bias is</p><p class=\"katexish\">plim β̂₁ = β₁ + γ · Cov(x,u)/Var(x)</p><p>So the bias has the <strong>sign of γ·π</strong> (the product of how u affects y and how u affects x). If both effects are positive, OLS <em>over</em>states β₁; if they have opposite signs it <em>under</em>states it.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> More data sharpens a <em>wrong</em> answer: the standard error shrinks around a biased point. Precision is not the same as correctness.</div><p>This is why we need a new strategy. We cannot fix endogeneity by collecting more rows — we need <em>extra information</em> in the form of an instrument, the subject of Module 2.</p>"},{"type":"code","title":"Watch OLS miss the truth","note":"True β₁ = 2. A hidden confounder u raises both x and y, so OLS overshoots — and tightening n does not help.","code":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n\ndef ols_slope(n):\n    u = rng.normal(0,1,n)                # hidden confounder\n    x = 1.0*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)        # u drives x\n    y = 1 + 2*x + 3*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)# u also drives y (true beta1=2)\n    return sm.OLS(y, sm.add_constant(x)).fit().params[1]\n\nns = [50,100,200,500,1000,2000]\nest = [ols_slope(n) for n in ns]\nfor n,b in zip(ns,est):\n    print(f'n={n:5d}   OLS beta1_hat = {b:.3f}   (true = 2.000)')\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.axhline(2,color='#c9c6ac',ls='--',lw=2,label='true beta1 = 2')\nplt.plot(ns,est,'o-',color='#da9100',lw=2,label='OLS estimate')\nplt.xlabel('sample size n'); plt.ylabel('beta1_hat')\nplt.title(f'OLS converges to {est[-1]:.2f}, not 2  (inconsistent)')\nplt.legend()"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Confounding slider","note":"γ controls how strongly the hidden u also pushes y. At γ = 0 there is no confounding and OLS is fine; crank it up and the estimate drifts away from the true 2.","params":[{"name":"gamma","label":"Confounder strength γ (effect of u on y)","min":-4,"max":4,"step":0.25,"value":3}],"template":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0); n=800\nu = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx = 1.0*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)\ny = 1 + 2*x + {{gamma}}*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)\nres = sm.OLS(y, sm.add_constant(x)).fit()\nb = res.params[1]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.scatter(x,y,s=10,color='#da9100',alpha=.4)\nxs=np.linspace(x.min(),x.max(),50)\nplt.plot(xs,res.params[0]+b*xs,color='#c9c6ac',lw=2,label='OLS fit')\nplt.plot(xs,res.params[0]+2*xs,color='#af983f',lw=2,ls='--',label='true slope=2')\nplt.legend(); plt.title(f'gamma={{gamma}}  ->  OLS beta1_hat = {b:.2f}  (bias {b-2:+.2f})')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: source of the bias","prompt":"An unobserved variable raises both schooling x and wages y. We regress wages on schooling by OLS. What happens as the sample grows very large?","choices":["The estimate converges to the true return to schooling","The estimate stays biased; only its standard error shrinks","The bias grows without bound","OLS becomes unbiased once n > 1000"],"answer":1,"hint":"Endogeneity is about consistency, not sampling noise.","explain":"Omitted-variable bias does not vanish with n. The point estimate converges to a wrong number while its SE shrinks around that wrong number — precise but inconsistent."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: sign of the bias","prompt":"In x = π·u + v and y = β₁·x + γ·u + η, suppose γ > 0 and π > 0. OLS will tend to…","choices":["overstate β₁","understate β₁","estimate β₁ exactly","give a negative β̂₁ regardless"],"answer":0,"hint":"The asymptotic bias has the sign of γ·π.","explain":"With γ·π > 0 the omitted u creates positive comovement between x and the error, so OLS attributes extra positive slope to x and overstates β₁."}]},{"id":"iv2sls-instruments","title":"2 · Instruments","summary":"A valid instrument is relevant (moves x) and excluded (affects y only through x).","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"What is an instrument?","html":"<h2>Borrowing clean variation</h2><p>An <strong>instrument</strong> z is a variable that nudges the endogenous regressor x but is otherwise unrelated to the outcome's error. If we can isolate the part of x that moves <em>because of z</em>, that part is clean — uncontaminated by the confounder — and we can use it to estimate β₁ honestly.</p><p>A valid instrument must satisfy two conditions:</p><ul><li><strong>Relevance.</strong> z actually shifts x: <code>Cov(z, x) ≠ 0</code>. This is testable — you can see it in the data (Module 4's first-stage F).</li><li><strong>Exclusion (exogeneity).</strong> z affects y <em>only</em> through x, and is uncorrelated with the error: <code>Cov(z, ε) = 0</code>. This is an assumption — it generally cannot be proven from data and must be argued from how the world works.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> z → x → y, with <em>no</em> arrow from z directly to y and no back-door from z to a confounder. The instrument's only path to the outcome runs through x.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Why both conditions are indispensable","html":"<h2>Break either leg and IV collapses</h2><p>The IV estimand can be written as a ratio of covariances:</p><p class=\"katexish\">β₁ = Cov(z, y) / Cov(z, x)</p><p>Read the two requirements straight off this formula:</p><ul><li>If <strong>relevance</strong> fails, <code>Cov(z,x) ≈ 0</code> and we are dividing by (almost) zero — the estimator explodes and becomes wildly imprecise. This is the <em>weak-instrument</em> problem.</li><li>If <strong>exclusion</strong> fails, z sneaks into the numerator through its own correlation with ε, and <code>Cov(z,y)</code> no longer reflects only the z→x→y path. The estimate is biased — often <em>worse</em> than OLS.</li></ul><p>Classic examples of plausible instruments:</p><ul><li>Quarter of birth as an instrument for years of schooling (compulsory-schooling laws), for the return to education.</li><li>Distance to the nearest college as an instrument for college attendance.</li><li>Rainfall as an instrument for crop output / income in conflict studies.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Relevance is checkable in your data; exclusion is a story you must defend. Reviewers attack the exclusion restriction, so make the argument explicit.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"See relevance vs exclusion in the data","note":"We can SEE relevance (z correlated with x). We assume exclusion (z uncorrelated with the structural error u). The plots make the difference concrete.","code":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0); n=600\nu = rng.normal(0,1,n)                 # confounder (unobserved error component)\nz = rng.normal(0,1,n)                 # instrument\nx = 1.2*z + 1.0*u + rng.normal(0,1,n) # z is RELEVANT for x\ny = 1 + 2*x + 3*u + rng.normal(0,1,n) # z enters y only through x (EXCLUSION holds)\n\nprint('corr(z, x)  =', round(np.corrcoef(z,x)[0,1], 3), ' <- relevance: should be far from 0')\nprint('corr(z, u)  =', round(np.corrcoef(z,u)[0,1], 3), ' <- exclusion: should be ~ 0')\nprint('corr(x, u)  =', round(np.corrcoef(x,u)[0,1], 3), ' <- x IS endogenous (nonzero)')\n\nfig,ax=plt.subplots(1,2,figsize=(9,3.7))\nax[0].scatter(z,x,s=8,color='#da9100',alpha=.4); ax[0].set_title('z vs x (relevant)'); ax[0].set_xlabel('z'); ax[0].set_ylabel('x')\nax[1].scatter(z,u,s=8,color='#c9c6ac',alpha=.4); ax[1].set_title('z vs u (excluded ~0)'); ax[1].set_xlabel('z'); ax[1].set_ylabel('u')\nfig.tight_layout()"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Relevance slider","note":"Slide the first-stage coefficient π (how strongly z moves x). Near 0 the instrument is irrelevant; the printed correlation tells you whether IV has anything to work with.","params":[{"name":"pi","label":"Instrument relevance π (effect of z on x)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.1,"value":1.2}],"template":"import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng=np.random.default_rng(0); n=600\nu=rng.normal(0,1,n); z=rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx={{pi}}*z + 1.0*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)\nr=np.corrcoef(z,x)[0,1]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.scatter(z,x,s=10,color='#da9100',alpha=.45)\nxs=np.linspace(z.min(),z.max(),50)\nplt.plot(xs,{{pi}}*xs,color='#c9c6ac',lw=2)\nplt.xlabel('instrument z'); plt.ylabel('regressor x')\nplt.title(f'pi={{pi}}   corr(z,x) = {r:.2f}'+('   (weak!)' if abs(r)<0.15 else ''))"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: which property is testable?","prompt":"Which of the two IV conditions can you directly check from the observed data?","choices":["Exclusion, by regressing y on z","Relevance, by checking that z is correlated with x","Both are fully testable","Neither can ever be examined"],"answer":1,"hint":"One condition involves the unobservable error.","explain":"Relevance (Cov(z,x) ≠ 0) is observable and testable via the first stage. Exclusion involves the unobservable error ε and must be argued from theory, not proven from data."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: a broken instrument","prompt":"A proposed instrument z is strongly correlated with x but ALSO has its own direct effect on y. Using it in IV will…","choices":["still give a consistent estimate of β₁","violate exclusion and generally yield a biased estimate","only inflate the standard errors","be fine as long as the first-stage F is large"],"answer":1,"hint":"z must affect y ONLY through x.","explain":"A direct z→y path violates the exclusion restriction, contaminating Cov(z,y). A huge first-stage F does not rescue an invalid instrument — it can make the bias more precise."}]},{"id":"iv2sls-2sls","title":"3 · Two-Stage Least Squares","summary":"Project x on the instruments, then regress y on the fitted x to recover a consistent β₁.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"The two stages","html":"<h2>2SLS in two moves</h2><p>Two-Stage Least Squares operationalises the instrument idea:</p><ul><li><strong>First stage.</strong> Regress the endogenous x on the instrument(s) z (and any exogenous controls):<br><code>x = π₀ + π₁·z + v</code>. Keep the fitted values x̂. These are the part of x <em>predicted by z</em> — the clean, exogenous slice.</li><li><strong>Second stage.</strong> Regress y on x̂ instead of x:<br><code>y = β₀ + β₁·x̂ + error</code>. Because x̂ is built only from z, it is uncorrelated with the confounder, so β̂₁ is consistent.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> x̂ strips out the contaminated variation in x and keeps only the variation z explains. Regressing on x̂ is like running the experiment z would have run for you.</div><p>With one endogenous regressor and one instrument the model is <em>just-identified</em> and 2SLS equals the simple IV ratio <code>Cov(z,y)/Cov(z,x)</code>. With more instruments than endogenous regressors it is <em>over-identified</em> (Module 4).</p>"},{"type":"read","title":"Do it in one call — and mind the SEs","html":"<h2>Use a built-in estimator</h2><p>You <em>can</em> run the two regressions by hand, but doing the second stage manually with <code>sm.OLS(y, x̂)</code> produces <strong>wrong standard errors</strong>: it ignores that x̂ is estimated, not observed. Use a dedicated estimator that corrects for this:</p><p class=\"katexish\">IV2SLS(endog, exog, instrument).fit()</p><p>The statsmodels signature (from <code>statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm</code>) is:</p><ul><li><code>endog</code> = y</li><li><code>exog</code> = the regressors in the structural equation — the constant <em>and</em> the endogenous x (plus any exogenous controls)</li><li><code>instrument</code> = the constant <em>and</em> the instrument z (plus the same exogenous controls, which instrument themselves)</li></ul><p>Build both design matrices with <code>sm.add_constant</code>. The point estimate matches the manual two-stage fit, but the reported SEs are now correct.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Manual two-stage = right coefficient, wrong SE. <code>IV2SLS</code> = right coefficient <em>and</em> right SE.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"OLS bias vs consistent 2SLS","note":"Same data, three estimators. OLS is pulled up by the confounder; the hand-built two-stage and IV2SLS both land near the true β₁ = 2. Note the manual second stage prints a WRONG SE.","code":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import IV2SLS\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0); n=1000\nu = rng.normal(0,1,n)                 # confounder\nz = rng.normal(0,1,n)                 # instrument\nx = 1.2*z + 1.0*u + rng.normal(0,1,n) # endogenous (shares u with y)\ny = 1 + 2*x + 3*u + rng.normal(0,1,n) # true beta1 = 2\n\n# OLS (biased)\nols = sm.OLS(y, sm.add_constant(x)).fit()\n# Manual two-stage: project x on z, then regress y on x-hat\nxhat = sm.OLS(x, sm.add_constant(z)).fit().fittedvalues\nman  = sm.OLS(y, sm.add_constant(xhat)).fit()\n# Proper IV2SLS (correct SEs)\nexog = sm.add_constant(x); instr = sm.add_constant(z)\niv   = IV2SLS(y, exog, instr).fit()\n\nprint(f'true beta1            = 2.000')\nprint(f'OLS    beta1_hat      = {ols.params[1]:.3f}   SE {ols.bse[1]:.3f}  (biased up)')\nprint(f'manual 2-stage beta1  = {man.params[1]:.3f}   (SE here is WRONG: {man.bse[1]:.3f})')\nprint(f'IV2SLS beta1_hat      = {iv.params[1]:.3f}   SE {iv.bse[1]:.3f}  (consistent, correct SE)')\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nlabels=['OLS','IV2SLS']; vals=[ols.params[1],iv.params[1]]; errs=[ols.bse[1],iv.bse[1]]\nplt.bar(labels,vals,yerr=errs,capsize=6,color=['#c9c6ac','#da9100'])\nplt.axhline(2,color='#af983f',ls='--',lw=2,label='true = 2'); plt.legend()\nplt.title(f'OLS {ols.params[1]:.2f}  vs  2SLS {iv.params[1]:.2f}')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Confounding slider: OLS drifts, 2SLS holds","note":"Increase γ (effect of the confounder on y). OLS marches away from 2; as long as the instrument stays valid, 2SLS stays anchored near the truth.","params":[{"name":"gamma","label":"Confounder strength γ","min":-4,"max":4,"step":0.25,"value":3}],"template":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import IV2SLS\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng=np.random.default_rng(0); n=1000\nu=rng.normal(0,1,n); z=rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx=1.2*z+1.0*u+rng.normal(0,1,n)\ny=1+2*x+{{gamma}}*u+rng.normal(0,1,n)\nols=sm.OLS(y,sm.add_constant(x)).fit().params[1]\niv =IV2SLS(y,sm.add_constant(x),sm.add_constant(z)).fit().params[1]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.bar(['OLS','2SLS'],[ols,iv],color=['#c9c6ac','#da9100'])\nplt.axhline(2,color='#af983f',ls='--',lw=2,label='true=2'); plt.legend()\nplt.title(f'gamma={{gamma}}:  OLS={ols:.2f}   2SLS={iv:.2f}')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: what goes into the second stage?","prompt":"In 2SLS, the second-stage regression of y is run on…","choices":["the raw endogenous x","the fitted values x̂ from regressing x on the instruments","the instrument z directly","the residuals from the first stage"],"answer":1,"hint":"We want the clean part of x that z explains.","explain":"The second stage uses x̂, the projection of x onto the instruments. Because x̂ is built only from exogenous z, it is uncorrelated with the confounder, giving a consistent β̂₁."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: why not hand-roll the SEs?","prompt":"Why prefer IV2SLS over manually running sm.OLS(y, x̂) for the second stage?","choices":["The manual coefficient is biased","The manual standard errors are wrong because they ignore that x̂ is estimated","IV2SLS uses a different, larger sample","There is no difference at all"],"answer":1,"hint":"The point estimate matches; something else does not.","explain":"Both give the same β̂₁, but plugging x̂ into a naive OLS understates uncertainty because it treats the generated regressor as if it were observed. IV2SLS corrects the variance."}]},{"id":"iv2sls-diagnostics","title":"4 · Diagnostics","summary":"First-stage F flags weak instruments; over-identification tests probe exclusion.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Weak instruments and the first-stage F","html":"<h2>How strong is your instrument?</h2><p>Relevance is not yes/no — it is a matter of degree. A <em>weak</em> instrument barely moves x, so <code>Cov(z,x)</code> is tiny and the IV ratio divides by near-zero. Consequences:</p><ul><li>2SLS standard errors balloon — estimates become wildly imprecise.</li><li>Worse, weak instruments reintroduce <strong>bias toward OLS</strong> in finite samples, and the usual SEs understate the true uncertainty.</li></ul><p>The standard diagnostic is the <strong>first-stage F-statistic</strong> for the excluded instruments — from <code>sm.OLS(x, sm.add_constant(z)).fit().fvalue</code>. A common rule of thumb (Staiger–Stock):</p><p class=\"katexish\">first-stage F ≳ 10  →  instrument is \"strong enough\"</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Always report the first-stage F. An F in the low single digits is a red flag: your 2SLS results may be unreliable no matter how clean the exclusion story is.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Over-identification: a partial check on exclusion","html":"<h2>When you have spare instruments</h2><p>If you have <em>more</em> valid instruments than endogenous regressors, the model is <strong>over-identified</strong>. Each instrument gives its own estimate of β₁; if all instruments are valid, those estimates should agree (up to sampling noise). An <strong>over-identification test</strong> (Sargan / Hansen J) checks exactly this.</p><p>The intuition: take the 2SLS residuals and regress them on all the instruments. Under a valid set, the instruments should have <em>no</em> explanatory power for the residual, so</p><p class=\"katexish\">J = n·R² ~ χ²(#instruments − #endogenous)</p><p>A <strong>small J / large p-value</strong> is consistent with valid instruments; a small p-value says at least one instrument fails exclusion (or the model is misspecified).</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Over-ID tests can only <em>reject</em> validity — passing is reassuring but not proof, and the test is silent in the just-identified case (one instrument, one endogenous regressor), where there is nothing to compare against.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Weak vs strong: F and 2SLS side by side","note":"Same structure, only the instrument's strength changes. Watch the first-stage F and the 2SLS estimate/SE move together.","code":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import IV2SLS\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0); n=1000\n\ndef run(pi):\n    u = rng.normal(0,1,n); z = rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    x = pi*z + 1.0*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    y = 1 + 2*x + 3*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)   # true beta1 = 2\n    F  = sm.OLS(x, sm.add_constant(z)).fit().fvalue\n    iv = IV2SLS(y, sm.add_constant(x), sm.add_constant(z)).fit()\n    return F, iv.params[1], iv.bse[1]\n\nprint(f\"{'pi':>5}{'first-F':>10}{'2SLS b1':>10}{'SE':>8}\")\npis=[0.05,0.1,0.3,0.8,1.5]; Fs=[]; bs=[]\nfor pi in pis:\n    F,b,se=run(pi); Fs.append(F); bs.append(b)\n    tag='  <-- weak (F<10)' if F<10 else ''\n    print(f'{pi:5.2f}{F:10.1f}{b:10.2f}{se:8.2f}{tag}')\n\nfig,ax=plt.subplots(1,2,figsize=(9,3.7))\nax[0].plot(pis,Fs,'o-',color='#da9100',lw=2); ax[0].axhline(10,color='#c9c6ac',ls='--')\nax[0].set_title('first-stage F'); ax[0].set_xlabel('pi')\nax[1].plot(pis,bs,'o-',color='#af983f',lw=2); ax[1].axhline(2,color='#c9c6ac',ls='--')\nax[1].set_title('2SLS beta1_hat'); ax[1].set_xlabel('pi')\nfig.tight_layout()"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Weak-instrument slider","note":"Drag π. The title shows the first-stage F and the 2SLS estimate. Below ~10 the estimate jumps around and the SE explodes — the weak-instrument danger zone.","params":[{"name":"pi","label":"Instrument strength π (z → x)","min":0.02,"max":1.5,"step":0.02,"value":0.1}],"template":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import IV2SLS\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng=np.random.default_rng(0); n=1000\nu=rng.normal(0,1,n); z=rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx={{pi}}*z+1.0*u+rng.normal(0,1,n)\ny=1+2*x+3*u+rng.normal(0,1,n)\nF=sm.OLS(x,sm.add_constant(z)).fit().fvalue\niv=IV2SLS(y,sm.add_constant(x),sm.add_constant(z)).fit()\nb,se=iv.params[1],iv.bse[1]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.errorbar([0],[b],yerr=[1.96*se],fmt='o',color='#da9100',capsize=8,ms=10,label='2SLS 95% CI')\nplt.axhline(2,color='#af983f',ls='--',lw=2,label='true=2')\nplt.xlim(-1,1); plt.xticks([]); plt.legend()\nweak=' WEAK' if F<10 else ' strong'\nplt.title(f'F={F:.1f}{weak}   2SLS b1={b:.2f}  SE={se:.2f}')"},{"type":"code","title":"Over-identification (Sargan) intuition","note":"Two instruments, one endogenous x. We compute the Sargan J statistic by regressing 2SLS residuals on the instruments. Valid instruments → large p-value; an instrument that leaks into y → tiny p-value.","code":"import numpy as np, statsmodels.api as sm\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import IV2SLS\nfrom scipy import stats\nrng = np.random.default_rng(3); n=1000\nu  = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nz1 = rng.normal(0,1,n); z2 = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx  = 0.8*z1 + 0.7*z2 + 1.0*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)\ny  = 1 + 2*x + 3*u + rng.normal(0,1,n)       # both instruments excluded -> valid\n\nexog  = sm.add_constant(x)\ninstr = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), z1, z2])\niv = IV2SLS(y, exog, instr).fit()\nprint('2SLS beta1_hat =', round(iv.params[1],3), ' (true 2)')\n\n# Sargan J: regress 2SLS residuals on ALL instruments; J = n*R^2 ~ chi2(#instr - #endog)\nresid = y - exog @ iv.params\naux = sm.OLS(resid, instr).fit()\nJ = n * aux.rsquared\ndof = instr.shape[1] - exog.shape[1]   # 3 instrument cols - 2 exog cols = 1\np = 1 - stats.chi2.cdf(J, dof)\nprint(f'Sargan J = {J:.3f}  dof={dof}  p-value = {p:.3f}')\nprint('large p-value -> no evidence against instrument validity (good)')\n\n# Now BREAK exclusion: let z2 leak directly into y\ny_bad = 1 + 2*x + 3*u + 1.5*z2 + rng.normal(0,1,n)\niv_b = IV2SLS(y_bad, exog, instr).fit()\nresid_b = y_bad - exog @ iv_b.params\nJ_b = n * sm.OLS(resid_b, instr).fit().rsquared\np_b = 1 - stats.chi2.cdf(J_b, dof)\nprint(f'\\nwith z2 invalid:  J = {J_b:.2f}  p-value = {p_b:.3f}  -> test REJECTS validity')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: reading the first-stage F","prompt":"Your first-stage F-statistic for the excluded instrument is 2.3. What is the appropriate concern?","choices":["The exclusion restriction is violated","The instrument is weak; 2SLS may be biased and very imprecise","The model is over-identified","Nothing — any positive F is fine"],"answer":1,"hint":"Compare against the rule-of-thumb threshold of about 10.","explain":"An F well below 10 signals a weak instrument: the first stage barely moves x, so 2SLS estimates are imprecise and biased toward OLS, with unreliable standard errors."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: the over-ID test","prompt":"With two instruments and one endogenous regressor, the Sargan/Hansen J test returns a very small p-value. This suggests…","choices":["the instruments are strong","at least one instrument likely violates the exclusion restriction","the first stage is weak","β₁ is exactly 2"],"answer":1,"hint":"The test compares the estimates implied by different instruments.","explain":"A small p-value rejects the null that all instruments are valid: the over-identifying instruments disagree, pointing to an exclusion violation (or misspecification). It cannot run in the just-identified case."}]}]},"did":{"id":"did","title":"Difference-in-Differences","modules":[{"id":"did-idea","title":"1 · The DiD idea","summary":"Why comparing one group's before/after change against a control group's change isolates a causal effect.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Two comparisons are better than one","html":"<h2>The problem DiD solves</h2><p>Suppose a state raises its minimum wage and we want the effect on employment. Two naive strategies both fail:</p><ul><li><strong>Before vs. after</strong> in the treated state alone: employment also moves because of the economy, seasons, trends — not just the policy.</li><li><strong>Treated vs. control</strong> at one point in time: the two states differ for many baseline reasons (industry mix, demographics) that have nothing to do with the policy.</li></ul><p>Each single comparison is contaminated. <strong>Difference-in-Differences (DiD)</strong> combines them: take the <em>change</em> in the treated group and subtract the <em>change</em> in a control group over the same window.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> The control group's change estimates what <em>would have happened</em> to the treated group anyway. Subtracting it strips out common trends and common shocks, leaving the causal effect.</div><h3>Four cells</h3><p>DiD lives on a 2×2 grid: {treated, control} × {before, after}. We need the average outcome in each of the four cells.</p>"},{"type":"read","title":"The estimator in words and symbols","html":"<h3>Differencing twice</h3><p>Let ȳ denote a group-period average outcome. The treated group's raw change over time is:</p><p class=\"katexish\">Δtreated = ȳ(treated, after) − ȳ(treated, before)</p><p>But part of that is just the trend everyone experienced. The control group reveals that trend:</p><p class=\"katexish\">Δcontrol = ȳ(control, after) − ȳ(control, before)</p><p>The DiD estimate is the <em>difference of these differences</em>:</p><p class=\"katexish\">ATT̂ = Δtreated − Δcontrol</p><p>This number estimates the <strong>Average Treatment effect on the Treated (ATT)</strong> — how much the policy changed outcomes for the units that actually got it.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> The control group is a <em>counterfactual machine</em>: it tells us the change the treated group would have had with no treatment. The leftover is the effect.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Compute DiD from four cell means","note":"Simulate a treated and a control group, before and after. True effect (ATT) is 4. We recover it by hand from the four averages.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 500\ntreated = rng.integers(0, 2, n)        # 1 = treated group, 0 = control\npost    = rng.integers(0, 2, n)        # 1 = after, 0 = before\nATT = 4.0\n# baseline gap 3 (treated higher), common time trend 2, true effect 4 only when treated & post\ny = 10 + 3*treated + 2*post + ATT*treated*post + rng.normal(0, 2, n)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(dict(y=y, treated=treated, post=post))\n\ncell = df.groupby(['treated','post'])['y'].mean().unstack()\nprint('Cell means (rows=treated, cols=post):')\nprint(cell.round(3))\n\nd_treated = cell.loc[1,1] - cell.loc[1,0]\nd_control = cell.loc[0,1] - cell.loc[0,0]\ndid = d_treated - d_control\nprint(f'\\nchange in treated group : {d_treated:.3f}')\nprint(f'change in control group: {d_control:.3f}')\nprint(f'DiD estimate (ATT)     : {did:.3f}   (true = {ATT})')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"See the four cells move","note":"Slide the true ATT and the common time trend. The DiD estimate should track the ATT and ignore the trend.","params":[{"name":"att","label":"True ATT","min":-6,"max":8,"step":0.5,"value":4},{"name":"trend","label":"Common time trend","min":-4,"max":6,"step":0.5,"value":2}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 600\ntreated = rng.integers(0,2,n); post = rng.integers(0,2,n)\ny = 10 + 3*treated + {{trend}}*post + {{att}}*treated*post + rng.normal(0,2,n)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(dict(y=y, treated=treated, post=post))\nc = df.groupby(['treated','post'])['y'].mean().unstack()\ndid = (c.loc[1,1]-c.loc[1,0]) - (c.loc[0,1]-c.loc[0,0])\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot([0,1],[c.loc[1,0],c.loc[1,1]],'o-',color='#da9100',lw=2,label='treated')\nplt.plot([0,1],[c.loc[0,0],c.loc[0,1]],'o-',color='#c9c6ac',lw=2,label='control')\n# counterfactual: treated baseline + control's change\ncf = c.loc[1,0] + (c.loc[0,1]-c.loc[0,0])\nplt.plot([0,1],[c.loc[1,0],cf],'--',color='#af983f',lw=2,label='treated counterfactual')\nplt.xticks([0,1],['before','after']); plt.legend(fontsize=7)\nplt.title(f'DiD (ATT) estimate = {did:.2f}')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"A common economy-wide shock makes BOTH groups' outcomes rise by 5 between before and after. What happens to the DiD estimate?","choices":["It rises by 5","It rises by 10","It is unchanged","It falls by 5"],"answer":2,"hint":"DiD subtracts the control group's change, which now contains that +5.","explain":"A shock common to both groups enters both Δtreated and Δcontrol, so it cancels in the difference of differences. DiD is designed to be immune to such common shocks."}]},{"id":"did-2x2","title":"2 · The 2×2 estimator","summary":"The DiD estimate is exactly the interaction coefficient in a regression of y on treated, post, and their product.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"DiD is one regression coefficient","html":"<h2>From four means to a regression</h2><p>Computing four cell means and differencing is intuitive, but a single regression gives the same number <em>plus</em> a standard error, a t-statistic, and the ability to add controls. We regress the outcome on two dummies and their interaction:</p><p class=\"katexish\">y = β₀ + β₁·treated + β₂·post + β₃·(treated·post) + ε</p><p>Read each coefficient off the 2×2 grid:</p><ul><li><strong>β₀</strong> = control group, before (the baseline cell).</li><li><strong>β₁</strong> = treated-vs-control gap <em>before</em> treatment (a level difference).</li><li><strong>β₂</strong> = before-vs-after change in the <em>control</em> group (the common trend).</li><li><strong>β₃</strong> = the <em>extra</em> change in the treated group on top of the trend — this is the <strong>DiD / ATT</strong>.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> The interaction term <code>treated:post</code> is the difference-in-differences. Its coefficient β₃ is the ATT, and its standard error gives you inference for free.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Why the interaction equals the double difference","html":"<h3>Plug in the four cells</h3><p>Evaluate the regression equation at each cell (ignoring ε, which averages to zero):</p><ul><li>control, before: ŷ = β₀</li><li>control, after: ŷ = β₀ + β₂</li><li>treated, before: ŷ = β₀ + β₁</li><li>treated, after: ŷ = β₀ + β₁ + β₂ + β₃</li></ul><p>Now form the double difference:</p><p class=\"katexish\">(treated,after − treated,before) − (control,after − control,before)</p><p class=\"katexish\">= (β₂ + β₃) − (β₂) = β₃</p><p>Everything except β₃ cancels. The regression is algebraically identical to the hand calculation — but it scales to many covariates and clustered standard errors.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> <code>smf.ols(\"y ~ treated*post\", df)</code> in statsmodels automatically adds <code>treated</code>, <code>post</code>, and <code>treated:post</code>. Just read off <code>params['treated:post']</code>.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Estimate the ATT with statsmodels","note":"Same simulated data as Module 1. The interaction coefficient matches the hand-computed DiD exactly, and now we get a standard error and p-value.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 500\ntreated = rng.integers(0,2,n); post = rng.integers(0,2,n)\nATT = 4.0\ny = 10 + 3*treated + 2*post + ATT*treated*post + rng.normal(0,2,n)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(dict(y=y, treated=treated, post=post))\n\nres = smf.ols('y ~ treated*post', df).fit()\nprint(res.summary().tables[1])\nprint()\nprint(f\"ATT (treated:post) = {res.params['treated:post']:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"std error          = {res.bse['treated:post']:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"true ATT           = {ATT}\")"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Noise vs. precision of the ATT","note":"Crank up the idiosyncratic noise σ and watch the point estimate stay roughly right while its standard error balloons.","params":[{"name":"att","label":"True ATT","min":-5,"max":8,"step":0.5,"value":4},{"name":"noise","label":"Noise σ","min":0.5,"max":8,"step":0.5,"value":2}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 600\ntreated = rng.integers(0,2,n); post = rng.integers(0,2,n)\ny = 10 + 3*treated + 2*post + {{att}}*treated*post + rng.normal(0,{{noise}},n)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(dict(y=y, treated=treated, post=post))\nres = smf.ols('y ~ treated*post', df).fit()\nb = res.params['treated:post']; se = res.bse['treated:post']\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.axhline({{att}}, color='#6f6b57', ls='--', lw=1.5, label='true ATT')\nplt.errorbar([0], [b], yerr=[1.96*se], fmt='o', color='#da9100', ecolor='#c9c6ac', capsize=6, ms=9, label='estimate +/- 95% CI')\nplt.xlim(-1,1); plt.xticks([]); plt.legend(fontsize=8)\nplt.title(f'ATT_hat = {b:.2f}   SE = {se:.3f}')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"In the regression y ~ treated*post, which coefficient is the causal DiD estimate (the ATT)?","choices":["The coefficient on 'treated'","The coefficient on 'post'","The coefficient on 'treated:post'","The intercept"],"answer":2,"hint":"Which coefficient survives the double difference after the others cancel?","explain":"Only the interaction 'treated:post' captures the extra change in the treated group beyond the common trend; the other terms cancel in the difference-of-differences."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"What does the coefficient on 'treated' (β₁) by itself represent?","choices":["The treatment effect","The pre-treatment level gap between treated and control groups","The common time trend","The post-period outcome of the control group"],"answer":1,"hint":"Set post = 0 and compare treated to control.","explain":"β₁ is the baseline (before-period) difference in levels between the groups. It is a fixed gap, not an effect — DiD explicitly allows the groups to differ in levels."}]},{"id":"did-parallel-trends","title":"3 · Parallel trends","summary":"DiD is valid only if the groups would have moved in parallel absent treatment; a pre-trend difference biases the ATT.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"The one assumption that matters","html":"<h2>Parallel trends</h2><p>DiD does not require the two groups to be identical — they can sit at different levels (that is β₁). What it <em>does</em> require is that, <strong>in the absence of treatment, the treated and control groups would have followed parallel paths over time.</strong></p><p class=\"katexish\">E[y(after) − y(before) | treated, no treatment] = E[y(after) − y(before) | control]</p><p>If this holds, the control group's change is exactly the counterfactual change for the treated group, and β₃ recovers the true ATT. If it fails, the control group's trend is the <em>wrong</em> counterfactual and the estimate is biased.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Parallel trends is about the <em>slopes</em> (changes), not the <em>levels</em>. Different starting points are fine; differently-shaped trajectories are fatal.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"What a violation does to the estimate","html":"<h3>Trend contamination</h3><p>Suppose the treated group was <em>already</em> growing faster than the control group before any treatment — say by δ per period (a <strong>differential pre-trend</strong>). Then even with zero true effect, the treated group's change exceeds the control's change purely from the divergent trend.</p><p>The DiD estimator cannot tell that divergence apart from a treatment effect, so it reports:</p><p class=\"katexish\">ATT̂ ≈ true ATT + (differential trend over the window)</p><p>A positive pre-trend inflates the estimate; a negative one deflates it. This is why a credible DiD study spends most of its effort <em>defending</em> parallel trends, typically by showing the groups moved together in the periods <em>before</em> treatment.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Parallel trends is untestable for the post period (we never observe the treated counterfactual). The standard evidence is indirect: equal trends in the <em>pre</em>-period make a parallel post-period plausible.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Inject a pre-trend and watch the bias","note":"True ATT is 4, but the treated group also drifts upward 1.5 per period faster than control. The naive 2×2 DiD over-states the effect.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\ntreated = rng.integers(0,2,n); post = rng.integers(0,2,n)\nATT = 4.0\nbias_per_period = 1.5   # treated grows faster regardless of treatment -> violates parallel trends\n# 'post' adds one period of the differential trend on top of the true effect\ny = (10 + 3*treated + 2*post\n     + bias_per_period*treated*post   # the offending differential trend\n     + ATT*treated*post               # the real effect\n     + rng.normal(0,2,n))\ndf = pd.DataFrame(dict(y=y, treated=treated, post=post))\nres = smf.ols('y ~ treated*post', df).fit()\nest = res.params['treated:post']\nprint(f'true ATT                 : {ATT}')\nprint(f'differential trend (bias): {bias_per_period}')\nprint(f'DiD estimate             : {est:.3f}')\nprint(f'bias                     : {est - ATT:+.3f}  (should be ~ +{bias_per_period})')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Dial the pre-trend violation","note":"Set the true ATT and a differential trend that breaks parallelism. The dashed line is what the treated group WOULD do under parallel trends; the gap to the orange observed line is the bias the estimator absorbs.","params":[{"name":"att","label":"True ATT","min":-4,"max":8,"step":0.5,"value":4},{"name":"violate","label":"Differential trend (violation)","min":-3,"max":5,"step":0.5,"value":0}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\ntreated = rng.integers(0,2,n); post = rng.integers(0,2,n)\ny = (10 + 3*treated + 2*post + {{violate}}*treated*post + {{att}}*treated*post + rng.normal(0,2,n))\ndf = pd.DataFrame(dict(y=y, treated=treated, post=post))\nres = smf.ols('y ~ treated*post', df).fit()\nest = res.params['treated:post']\nc = df.groupby(['treated','post'])['y'].mean().unstack()\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot([0,1],[c.loc[1,0],c.loc[1,1]],'o-',color='#da9100',lw=2,label='treated (observed)')\nplt.plot([0,1],[c.loc[0,0],c.loc[0,1]],'o-',color='#c9c6ac',lw=2,label='control')\ncf = c.loc[1,0] + (c.loc[0,1]-c.loc[0,0])\nplt.plot([0,1],[c.loc[1,0],cf],'--',color='#af983f',lw=2,label='parallel-trends counterfactual')\nplt.xticks([0,1],['before','after']); plt.legend(fontsize=7)\nplt.title(f'ATT_hat = {est:.2f}  (true {{att}}, bias {est-{{att}}:+.2f})')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"The treated group sits 8 points above the control group in BOTH the before and after periods, and both rise by 3 over time. Is parallel trends satisfied?","choices":["No, the 8-point gap violates it","Yes, the gap is constant so the trends are parallel","Cannot tell without the true ATT","No, because they are not at the same level"],"answer":1,"hint":"Parallel trends concerns changes over time, not the level gap.","explain":"A constant level gap with equal changes (both +3) means the trends are parallel. DiD allows different levels; it only needs equal trends."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"If the treated group was already trending up faster than control before treatment, the standard 2×2 DiD estimate of a positive ATT will tend to be:","choices":["Unbiased","Biased upward (too large)","Biased downward (too small)","Exactly zero"],"answer":1,"hint":"The estimator mistakes the extra pre-existing growth for treatment effect.","explain":"A positive differential pre-trend adds to the treated group's change, and DiD attributes that extra change to treatment, overstating the ATT."}]},{"id":"did-event-study","title":"4 · Event-study / dynamic DiD","summary":"Estimate a separate effect for each period relative to treatment to test pre-trends and trace the effect's dynamics.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"From one number to a whole path","html":"<h2>The event-study design</h2><p>The 2×2 estimator collapses everything into a single before/after contrast. With a panel of many periods we can do better: estimate a <strong>separate treated-vs-control gap for every period</strong>, relative to a chosen baseline period (usually the last period before treatment).</p><p>We interact the treated dummy with a full set of time dummies. Picking period <em>r</em> as the reference, the coefficient on <code>treatedₜ</code> is:</p><p class=\"katexish\">θₜ = (treated − control gap in period t) − (treated − control gap in period r)</p><p>Plotting θₜ against t gives the <strong>event-study plot</strong>, the single most important diagnostic in modern DiD.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Pre-treatment coefficients (t &lt; treatment) should be near zero — that is your visual test of parallel trends. Post-treatment coefficients trace how the effect turns on and evolves.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Reading leads and lags","html":"<h3>Leads, lags, and the reference period</h3><ul><li><strong>Leads</strong> (periods before treatment, t &lt; T*): these are a falsification test. If parallel trends holds, the treated and control groups tracked each other beforehand, so the leads should hug zero. Leads that drift away from zero signal a pre-trend and undercut the design.</li><li><strong>Reference period</strong> (t = T*−1): normalized to exactly 0 by construction. Every other coefficient is measured relative to it, so it has no error bar.</li><li><strong>Lags</strong> (periods at/after treatment): the dynamic treatment effects. They might jump immediately, ramp up gradually, or fade out.</li></ul><p>In statsmodels we use a panel and the formula <code>y ~ C(time, Treatment(reference=r))*treated + C(unit)</code>, then read off the coefficients named <code>...:treated</code>. The <code>C(unit)</code> term adds unit fixed effects, absorbing every time-invariant difference between units. (The standalone <code>treated</code> term is then collinear with the unit dummies and contributes nothing extra — the per-period interactions carry the identification.)</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Flat pre-period coefficients are necessary, not sufficient — but a clean event-study plot with near-zero leads is the strongest practical evidence a DiD can offer.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Build a panel and run an event study","note":"Multi-period panel: treatment turns on at time 4 with true effect 3. We estimate per-period effects and plot leads & lags with 95% intervals. Pre-period coefficients should sit near zero.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nT, nunit, treat_time, ref = 8, 60, 4, 3\nrows = []\nfor u in range(nunit):\n    g = 1 if u < nunit//2 else 0        # first half treated\n    ai = rng.normal(0, 1)               # unit fixed effect\n    for t in range(T):\n        eff = 3.0 if (g == 1 and t >= treat_time) else 0.0\n        y = 5 + ai + 0.5*t + eff + rng.normal(0, 1)\n        rows.append((u, t, g, y))\ndf = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=['unit','time','treated','y'])\n\nres = smf.ols(f'y ~ C(time, Treatment(reference={ref}))*treated + C(unit)', df).fit()\nts, est, se = [], [], []\nfor t in range(T):\n    ts.append(t)\n    if t == ref:\n        est.append(0.0); se.append(0.0); continue\n    k = f'C(time, Treatment(reference={ref}))[T.{t}]:treated'\n    est.append(res.params[k]); se.append(res.bse[k])\n\nfor t,e in zip(ts,est): print(f'period {t}: effect = {e:+.3f}')\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.errorbar(ts, est, yerr=[1.96*s for s in se], fmt='o-', color='#da9100', ecolor='#c9c6ac', capsize=3)\nplt.axhline(0, color='#6f6b57', lw=1)\nplt.axvline(treat_time-0.5, color='#af983f', ls='--', label='treatment starts')\nplt.xlabel('period'); plt.ylabel('treated - control gap'); plt.legend(fontsize=8)\nplt.title('Event study: leads near 0, lags near +3')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Pre-trend vs. true effect in the event-study plot","note":"Add a true post-treatment ATT and a per-period pre-trend that breaks parallelism. Watch the LEADS lift off zero when you introduce a pre-trend — exactly the warning sign analysts look for.","params":[{"name":"att","label":"True ATT (post)","min":-2,"max":6,"step":0.5,"value":3},{"name":"pretrend","label":"Differential trend per period","min":-1,"max":1.5,"step":0.1,"value":0}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nT, nunit, treat_time, ref = 8, 60, 4, 3\nrows = []\nfor u in range(nunit):\n    g = 1 if u < nunit//2 else 0\n    ai = rng.normal(0,1)\n    for t in range(T):\n        eff = {{att}} if (g==1 and t>=treat_time) else 0.0\n        trend = {{pretrend}}*t*g          # divergence in every period -> shows up in leads too\n        y = 5 + ai + 0.5*t + trend + eff + rng.normal(0,1)\n        rows.append((u,t,g,y))\ndf = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=['unit','time','treated','y'])\nres = smf.ols(f'y ~ C(time, Treatment(reference={ref}))*treated + C(unit)', df).fit()\nts, est, se = [], [], []\nfor t in range(T):\n    ts.append(t)\n    if t == ref:\n        est.append(0.0); se.append(0.0); continue\n    k = f'C(time, Treatment(reference={ref}))[T.{t}]:treated'\n    est.append(res.params[k]); se.append(res.bse[k])\npre = [est[t] for t in range(treat_time)]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.errorbar(ts, est, yerr=[1.96*s for s in se], fmt='o-', color='#da9100', ecolor='#c9c6ac', capsize=3)\nplt.axhline(0, color='#6f6b57', lw=1)\nplt.axvline(treat_time-0.5, color='#af983f', ls='--', label='treatment starts')\nplt.xlabel('period'); plt.ylabel('treated - control gap'); plt.legend(fontsize=8)\nplt.title(f'max |lead| = {max(abs(p) for p in pre):.2f}  (should be ~0 if valid)')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"In an event-study plot, what is the purpose of inspecting the PRE-treatment (lead) coefficients?","choices":["They measure the treatment effect","They test the parallel-trends assumption — they should be near zero","They set the reference period","They measure how fast the effect fades out"],"answer":1,"hint":"Before treatment there can be no effect, so any nonzero value means something else is going on.","explain":"Leads capture treated-vs-control differences before treatment, where the effect must be zero. Near-zero leads support parallel trends; nonzero leads reveal a pre-trend that invalidates the design."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"Why is the reference period's coefficient exactly 0 with no confidence interval?","choices":["The effect happens to be zero there","Every other period is measured relative to it, so it is normalized to 0 by construction","Its standard error is too large to plot","Treatment has no effect in that period"],"answer":1,"hint":"All θₜ are differences relative to that one omitted period.","explain":"The reference (omitted) period is the baseline against which all other coefficients are defined, so it is mechanically 0 and carries no sampling uncertainty."}]}]},"var":{"id":"var","title":"Vector Autoregression (VAR)","modules":[{"id":"var-from-ar1-to-var","title":"1 · From AR(1) to VAR (joint dynamics)","summary":"How stacking several AR equations into one system lets each variable depend on the lagged values of all the others.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"One series at a time: AR(1)","html":"<h2>Recap: the AR(1) model</h2><p>A single time series <em>y</em> follows an <strong>autoregression of order 1</strong> when today's value is a constant plus a fraction of yesterday's value plus noise:</p><p class=\"katexish\">yₜ = c + φ·yₜ₋₁ + εₜ</p><p>The coefficient <code>φ</code> is the <em>persistence</em>: if φ = 0.8, a shock today still leaves 0.8 of itself tomorrow, 0.64 the day after, and so on, decaying geometrically. The series is <strong>stationary</strong> (it doesn't wander off to infinity) precisely when <code>|φ| &lt; 1</code>.</p><h3>The problem AR(1) ignores</h3><p>Real economic variables move <em>together</em>. Interest rates respond to inflation; inflation responds to past output; output responds to past rates. An AR(1) on inflation alone pretends inflation only listens to its own past — it is deaf to everything else. We need a model where each variable can depend on the recent past of <em>every</em> variable.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> A VAR is just several AR equations estimated jointly, where the right-hand side of <em>every</em> equation contains the lags of <em>every</em> variable in the system.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Stacking equations into a VAR(1)","html":"<h2>The VAR(1) system</h2><p>Take two series, <code>y1</code> and <code>y2</code>. A VAR(1) writes one equation for each:</p><p class=\"katexish\">y1ₜ = c₁ + a₁₁·y1ₜ₋₁ + a₁₂·y2ₜ₋₁ + ε1ₜ</p><p class=\"katexish\">y2ₜ = c₂ + a₂₁·y1ₜ₋₁ + a₂₂·y2ₜ₋₁ + ε2ₜ</p><p>Collect the variables into a vector <code>Yₜ = (y1ₜ, y2ₜ)′</code> and the coefficients into a 2×2 matrix <strong>A</strong>. The whole system becomes one compact line:</p><p class=\"katexish\">Yₜ = c + A·Yₜ₋₁ + εₜ</p><p>The matrix entries have crisp meanings:</p><ul><li><strong>Diagonal</strong> <code>a₁₁, a₂₂</code> — each variable's own <em>persistence</em> (like φ in AR(1)).</li><li><strong>Off-diagonal</strong> <code>a₁₂, a₂₁</code> — the <em>spillover</em> from one variable's past into the other's present. These are exactly what a single-equation AR throws away.</li></ul><h3>When is a VAR stable?</h3><p>The univariate rule <code>|φ| &lt; 1</code> generalizes: a VAR(1) is stationary when every <strong>eigenvalue of A</strong> has modulus less than 1. The eigenvalues are the multivariate analogue of φ — they govern how shocks decay through the coupled system.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Diagonal = memory of yourself; off-diagonal = how you push the other variable around. Stability is governed by the eigenvalues of <strong>A</strong>, not the individual entries.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Simulate and recover a VAR(1)","note":"We build data from a known 2×2 matrix A, then let statsmodels recover it. Compare the printed coefficients to the true A.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n\n# True coefficient matrix: strong own-persistence, modest spillovers\nA = np.array([[0.6, 0.2],\n              [0.1, 0.5]])\nprint('eigenvalues |.| =', np.round(np.abs(np.linalg.eigvals(A)), 3), '(all < 1 => stable)')\n\nn = 1000\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1', 'y2'])\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=1)\n\nprint('\\nTrue A:\\n', A)\nprint('\\nEstimated A (res.coefs[0]):\\n', np.round(res.coefs[0], 3))\nprint('\\nFull readable summary:')\nprint(res.summary())"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Watch the two series co-move","note":"Push up the spillover a₂₁ and see how a wiggle in y1 starts dragging y2 along. Keep diagonals < 1 for stability.","params":[{"name":"a11","label":"y1 persistence a₁₁","min":0,"max":0.95,"step":0.05,"value":0.6},{"name":"a22","label":"y2 persistence a₂₂","min":0,"max":0.95,"step":0.05,"value":0.5},{"name":"a21","label":"spillover y1→y2 (a₂₁)","min":-0.6,"max":0.6,"step":0.05,"value":0.1}],"template":"import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA = np.array([[{{a11}}, 0.2],\n              [{{a21}}, {{a22}}]])\nev = np.max(np.abs(np.linalg.eigvals(A)))\nn = 300\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(Y[:,0], color='#da9100', lw=1.3, label='y1')\nplt.plot(Y[:,1], color='#6f6b57', lw=1.3, label='y2')\nplt.legend(loc='upper right', fontsize=8)\nstab = 'STABLE' if ev < 1 else 'EXPLOSIVE'\nplt.title(f'max|eigenvalue| = {ev:.2f}  ->  {stab}')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: what makes it a VAR?","prompt":"In the VAR(1) matrix A, what does a non-zero off-diagonal entry a₁₂ represent?","choices":["The persistence of y1 (how much of its own past it keeps)","The effect of last period's y2 on this period's y1","The contemporaneous correlation of the two error terms","The long-run mean of y1"],"answer":1,"hint":"Read off the first equation: y1ₜ = c₁ + a₁₁·y1ₜ₋₁ + a₁₂·y2ₜ₋₁ + ε1ₜ.","explain":"a₁₂ multiplies the lagged value of the OTHER variable (y2ₜ₋₁) in the equation for y1, so it is the cross-variable spillover; own-persistence lives on the diagonal."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: stability","prompt":"A VAR(1) is stationary (stable) when…","choices":["every entry of A is less than 1 in absolute value","the determinant of A is positive","every eigenvalue of A has modulus less than 1","the off-diagonal entries are zero"],"answer":2,"hint":"It is the multivariate version of the AR(1) rule |φ| < 1.","explain":"Stationarity depends on the eigenvalues of A, not the raw entries — a matrix with large entries can still be stable, and one with small entries can be explosive."}]},{"id":"var-estimation-lag-selection","title":"2 · Estimation & lag selection","summary":"How a VAR is fit equation-by-equation with OLS, and how information criteria pick the lag length p.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Estimating a VAR is just OLS, k times","html":"<h2>Each equation is its own regression</h2><p>A VAR(p) regresses each variable on <code>p</code> lags of <em>all</em> variables. Because every equation has the <em>same</em> right-hand-side regressors (the stacked lags), running OLS separately on each equation is fully efficient — there is nothing to gain from a fancier joint estimator. So fitting a k-variable VAR(p) is literally <strong>k ordinary OLS regressions</strong>.</p><p>What ties the equations together is not the estimation but the <strong>residual covariance matrix</strong> <code>Σ</code>. The shocks <code>ε1ₜ</code> and <code>ε2ₜ</code> are usually correlated <em>within</em> a period (a surprise that hits output often hits inflation the same quarter). That contemporaneous correlation lives entirely in Σ and becomes crucial later for impulse responses.</p><h3>Choosing the lag length p</h3><p>How many lags? Too few and you miss real dynamics (biased, autocorrelated residuals); too many and you burn degrees of freedom estimating noise (a VAR(p) on k variables has <code>k·(k·p+1)</code> parameters — it grows fast). We trade fit against parsimony with <strong>information criteria</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>AIC</strong> — light penalty, tends to pick more lags; good for forecasting.</li><li><strong>BIC</strong> (a.k.a. SC) — heavy penalty (∝ log n), picks fewer lags; consistent for the true order.</li><li><strong>HQIC</strong> — in between.</li><li><strong>FPE</strong> — final prediction error, close to AIC.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Fit = k separate OLS regressions. Lag choice = minimize an information criterion that rewards fit and punishes the parameter count.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"select_order: read the criteria table","note":"We simulate a true VAR(2) and print the IC table. Each criterion reports the lag it would choose. Watch BIC pick fewer lags than AIC.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n\n# True process is VAR(2): A1 then A2 act on successive lags\nA1 = np.array([[0.5, 0.1],[0.0, 0.5]])\nA2 = np.array([[0.0, 0.2],[0.2, 0.0]])\nn = 1000\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(2, n):\n    Y[t] = A1 @ Y[t-1] + A2 @ Y[t-2] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\n\nsel = VAR(df).select_order(maxlags=8)\nprint(sel.summary())\nprint('\\nLag chosen by each criterion:')\nprint(sel.selected_orders)"},{"type":"code","title":"Fit by an information criterion automatically","note":"Pass ic='aic' and statsmodels searches lags up to maxlags and keeps the best. Then we sanity-check the residuals are close to white noise.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nfrom statsmodels.stats.stattools import durbin_watson\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA1 = np.array([[0.5, 0.1],[0.0, 0.5]])\nA2 = np.array([[0.0, 0.2],[0.2, 0.0]])\nn = 1000\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(2, n):\n    Y[t] = A1 @ Y[t-1] + A2 @ Y[t-2] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\n\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=8, ic='aic')\nprint('Lag order chosen by AIC:', res.k_ar)\nprint('Shape of coefficient array res.coefs (p, k, k):', res.coefs.shape)\nprint('\\nResidual covariance Sigma:\\n', np.round(res.sigma_u, 3))\nprint('\\nDurbin-Watson per equation (near 2 = no leftover autocorrelation):')\nprint(np.round(durbin_watson(res.resid.values), 2))"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Sample size vs the lag BIC picks","note":"The data are truly VAR(2). With few observations, criteria get noisy; as n grows, BIC should home in on lag 2. Move the slider and read the chosen lags in the title.","params":[{"name":"n","label":"sample size n","min":80,"max":1000,"step":20,"value":200}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA1 = np.array([[0.5, 0.1],[0.0, 0.5]])\nA2 = np.array([[0.0, 0.2],[0.2, 0.0]])\nn = int({{n}})\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(2, n):\n    Y[t] = A1 @ Y[t-1] + A2 @ Y[t-2] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\nmaxlags = 8\nsel = VAR(df).select_order(maxlags=maxlags)\naics, bics = [], []\nbase = VAR(df)\nfor p in range(1, maxlags+1):\n    r = base.fit(p)\n    aics.append(r.aic); bics.append(r.bic)\nlags = np.arange(1, maxlags+1)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(lags, aics, 'o-', color='#da9100', label='AIC')\nplt.plot(lags, bics, 's-', color='#6f6b57', label='BIC')\nplt.axvline(2, color='#c9c6ac', ls='--', lw=1)\nplt.xlabel('lag p'); plt.legend(fontsize=8)\nplt.title(f\"n={n}: AIC picks {sel.selected_orders['aic']}, BIC picks {sel.selected_orders['bic']} (truth=2)\")"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: which criterion?","prompt":"You care most about not overfitting and want the most parsimonious lag order that is consistent for the true p. Which criterion do you lean on?","choices":["AIC","BIC (SC)","FPE","always use the maximum lag"],"answer":1,"hint":"One of these has a penalty that grows with log(n) and is the strictest.","explain":"BIC's penalty (∝ log n · #params) is the harshest, so it selects fewer lags and is consistent for the true order; AIC/FPE favor richer models better suited to pure forecasting."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: estimation method","prompt":"Why can a VAR be estimated by running OLS separately on each equation without losing efficiency?","choices":["Because the errors are independent across equations","Because every equation shares exactly the same set of regressors (the stacked lags)","Because OLS is always efficient","Because the residual covariance Σ is diagonal"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think about what SUR/GLS would buy you when all equations have identical regressors.","explain":"When every equation has the same regressors, the GLS (SUR) estimator collapses to equation-by-equation OLS, so separate OLS is already fully efficient even though the errors are correlated across equations."}]},{"id":"var-irf-fevd","title":"3 · Impulse-response functions & FEVD","summary":"Tracing how a one-time shock to one variable ripples through the system over time, and how to split forecast uncertainty across shocks.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"What an impulse response answers","html":"<h2>Impulse-response functions (IRFs)</h2><p>The coefficient matrix A is hard to read directly. The <strong>impulse-response function</strong> turns it into a story: <em>if variable j gets a one-time, one-unit shock today and nothing else happens, what is the path of every variable over the next h periods?</em></p><p>Mechanically, the response at horizon h is just powers of A acting on the shock. For a VAR(1) a shock <code>δ</code> hitting at time 0 produces responses <code>δ, A·δ, A²·δ, A³·δ, …</code> — the system's memory unrolling. Because A is stable, these fade to zero: shocks are <em>transitory</em>.</p><h3>Why orthogonalize?</h3><p>There's a catch. The raw shocks <code>ε1</code> and <code>ε2</code> are <em>correlated</em> (that's Σ from Module 2). A 'shock to y1 alone' is then ill-defined — in the data, y1 surprises and y2 surprises arrive together. To get clean, isolated shocks we <strong>orthogonalize</strong> using a Cholesky factor of Σ. This imposes an ordering: the first variable can affect the second contemporaneously, but not vice-versa within the same period.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> <code>orth_irfs</code> gives <em>uncorrelated</em> structural shocks via the Cholesky factor of Σ, but the answer depends on variable ordering. Reorder the columns and the impact responses can change.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Plot the four orthogonalized IRFs","note":"We simulate CORRELATED shocks (corr 0.6) so Σ is non-diagonal and orthogonalization actually does something. orth_irfs is indexed [horizon, response variable, shock variable]; we draw all 4 paths and print the impact matrix.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA = np.array([[0.6, 0.2],[0.1, 0.5]])\n# Correlated structural shocks: L is a Cholesky factor of a 0.6-correlation matrix\nL = np.linalg.cholesky(np.array([[1.0, 0.6],[0.6, 1.0]]))\nn = 1000\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + L @ rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=1)\nprint('Residual covariance Sigma (note the non-zero off-diagonal):\\n', np.round(res.sigma_u, 3))\n\nH = 12\nirf = res.irf(H)\no = irf.orth_irfs   # shape (H+1, 2, 2): [horizon, response, shock]\nh = np.arange(H+1)\nnames = ['y1','y2']\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(6, 4.4), sharex=True)\nfor sh in range(2):      # which variable is shocked\n    for rp in range(2):  # which variable responds\n        a = ax[rp, sh]\n        a.axhline(0, color='#c9c6ac', lw=.8)\n        a.plot(h, o[:, rp, sh], color='#da9100', lw=1.8)\n        a.set_title(f'{names[sh]} shock -> {names[rp]}', fontsize=8)\nfor a in ax[1]:\n    a.set_xlabel('horizon')\nfig.tight_layout()\nprint('\\nImpact (h=0) matrix [response x shock]:\\n', np.round(o[0], 3))"},{"type":"code","title":"FEVD: who drives forecast uncertainty?","note":"With correlated shocks the cross-shares are meaningful. The forecast-error variance decomposition splits each variable's h-step forecast variance into shares per shock; shares sum to 1 across each row.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA = np.array([[0.6, 0.2],[0.1, 0.5]])\nL = np.linalg.cholesky(np.array([[1.0, 0.6],[0.6, 1.0]]))\nn = 1000\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + L @ rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=1)\n\nfevd = res.fevd(10)\nprint(fevd.summary())\n# decomp[i] is the (horizons x k) table for variable i: cols = shock shares\nshare_y1_from_y2 = fevd.decomp[0][-1, 1]\nprint(f'\\nAt horizon 10, share of y1 forecast variance due to the y2 shock: {share_y1_from_y2:.3f}')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Slide persistence & spillover, redraw the IRF","note":"Shocks are correlated (corr 0.6). This redraws the response of BOTH variables to a single orthogonalized shock in y1. More persistence (a₁₁) => slower decay; more spillover (a₂₁) => bigger hump in y2's response.","params":[{"name":"a11","label":"y1 persistence a₁₁","min":0,"max":0.9,"step":0.05,"value":0.6},{"name":"a21","label":"spillover y1->y2 (a₂₁)","min":-0.6,"max":0.6,"step":0.05,"value":0.2}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA = np.array([[{{a11}}, 0.2],[{{a21}}, 0.5]])\nL = np.linalg.cholesky(np.array([[1.0, 0.6],[0.6, 1.0]]))\nn = 600\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + L @ rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=1)\nH = 12\no = res.irf(H).orth_irfs   # [horizon, response, shock]\nh = np.arange(H+1)\npeak_y2 = np.max(np.abs(o[:, 1, 0]))\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.axhline(0, color='#c9c6ac', lw=.8)\nplt.plot(h, o[:, 0, 0], color='#da9100', lw=2, label='y1 response')\nplt.plot(h, o[:, 1, 0], color='#6f6b57', lw=2, label='y2 response')\nplt.legend(fontsize=8); plt.xlabel('horizon')\nplt.title(f'Response to a y1 shock | peak |y2 response| = {peak_y2:.2f}')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: ordering matters","prompt":"You compute orthogonalized IRFs, then swap the order of the two columns in your DataFrame and recompute. What can change?","choices":["Nothing — orthogonalization is order-invariant","The contemporaneous (impact) responses, because the Cholesky ordering changes which variable can affect the other within the same period","Only the long-run responses","The estimated coefficient matrix A"],"answer":1,"hint":"Cholesky imposes a recursive timing assumption based on column order.","explain":"Orthogonalization via Cholesky assumes earlier-ordered variables can hit later ones contemporaneously but not the reverse; changing the order changes that assumption and hence the impact responses."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: reading FEVD","prompt":"A FEVD says that at horizon 10, 85% of y1's forecast-error variance comes from its own shock and 15% from the y2 shock. This means…","choices":["y1 is mostly driven by its own innovations at that horizon","y2 Granger-causes y1 with p < 0.15","the IRF of y1 to a y2 shock is exactly 0.15","y1 and y2 are uncorrelated"],"answer":0,"hint":"FEVD shares are about variance attribution, and they sum to 1 across shocks for each variable.","explain":"FEVD splits a variable's forecast uncertainty into shares per shock; 85% own-share means y1's unpredictability is mostly self-generated at horizon 10, with y2 contributing only 15%."}]},{"id":"var-granger-forecasting","title":"4 · Granger causality & forecasting","summary":"Testing whether one variable's past helps predict another, and producing and reading multi-step VAR forecasts.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Granger causality: prediction, not philosophy","html":"<h2>Does y2's past help predict y1?</h2><p><strong>Granger causality</strong> has a deliberately modest definition: <em>y2 Granger-causes y1</em> if the past of y2 contains information that improves the forecast of y1, beyond what y1's own past already provides. It is a statement about <strong>predictive content</strong>, not about true cause and effect — y2 could merely be a leading indicator.</p><p>In a VAR this becomes a clean joint test. y2 fails to Granger-cause y1 exactly when <em>all</em> the coefficients on lagged y2 in the y1 equation are zero:</p><p class=\"katexish\">H₀: a₁₂⁽¹⁾ = a₁₂⁽²⁾ = … = a₁₂⁽ᵖ⁾ = 0</p><p>That's an F-test (or Wald χ²) on a block of coefficients. A small p-value rejects H₀ → y2 <em>does</em> carry predictive information about y1.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Granger causality = 'past of X improves the forecast of Y'. It is a block-of-zeros F-test on the off-diagonal lag coefficients, and it is about prediction, not deep causation.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Run the causality test both directions","note":"We build data where y1's past genuinely drives y2 but NOT the reverse. The test should reject in one direction and not the other.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n\n# y2 listens to y1 (a21=0.4), but y1 ignores y2 (a12=0)\nA = np.array([[0.5, 0.0],\n              [0.4, 0.5]])\nn = 1000\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=1)\n\nt1 = res.test_causality('y2', ['y1'], kind='f')  # does y1 help predict y2?\nt2 = res.test_causality('y1', ['y2'], kind='f')  # does y2 help predict y1?\nprint('y1 -> y2 :  p =', round(t1.pvalue, 4), '=>', 'REJECT H0 (y1 helps)' if t1.pvalue < 0.05 else 'cannot reject')\nprint('y2 -> y1 :  p =', round(t2.pvalue, 4), '=>', 'REJECT H0 (y2 helps)' if t2.pvalue < 0.05 else 'cannot reject')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Spillover strength vs the Granger p-value","note":"Crank a₂₁ (how much y1's past feeds into y2). As the true spillover grows, the test for 'y1 → y2' should reject ever more decisively (tiny p-value).","params":[{"name":"a21","label":"true spillover y1->y2 (a₂₁)","min":0,"max":0.6,"step":0.02,"value":0}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA = np.array([[0.5, 0.0],[{{a21}}, 0.5]])\nn = 500\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=1)\np = res.test_causality('y2', ['y1'], kind='f').pvalue\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(Y[:150,0], color='#da9100', lw=1.2, label='y1')\nplt.plot(Y[:150,1], color='#6f6b57', lw=1.2, label='y2')\nplt.legend(fontsize=8, loc='upper right')\nverdict = 'REJECT H0' if p < 0.05 else 'cannot reject'\nplt.title(f'Granger y1->y2:  p = {p:.4f}  ({verdict})')"},{"type":"code","title":"Forecast h steps ahead with bands","note":"forecast_interval() needs the last p observations as a seed and returns the point forecast plus lower/upper bands. We plot history, the point forecast, and an approximate 95% interval.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.api import VAR\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nA = np.array([[0.6, 0.2],[0.1, 0.5]])\nn = 400\nY = np.zeros((n, 2))\nfor t in range(1, n):\n    Y[t] = A @ Y[t-1] + rng.normal(0, 1, 2)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(Y, columns=['y1','y2'])\nres = VAR(df).fit(maxlags=1)\nlags = res.k_ar\n\nsteps = 15\n# point forecast + analytic forecast-error interval\nmid, lower, upper = res.forecast_interval(df.values[-lags:], steps, alpha=0.05)\nprint('First 3 forecast rows [y1, y2]:\\n', np.round(mid[:3], 3))\n\nfut = np.arange(n, n+steps)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(np.arange(n-40, n), Y[-40:,0], color='#6f6b57', lw=1.2, label='y1 history')\nplt.plot(fut, mid[:,0], color='#da9100', lw=2, label='y1 forecast')\nplt.fill_between(fut, lower[:,0], upper[:,0], color='#af983f', alpha=.3, label='95% band')\nplt.axvline(n, color='#c9c6ac', ls='--', lw=1)\nplt.legend(fontsize=8); plt.title('VAR forecast of y1 with 95% interval')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: what Granger causality is (and isn't)","prompt":"A test finds that y2 Granger-causes y1 (p = 0.001). The safest interpretation is:","choices":["Changing y2 will mechanically change y1 — true causation is proven","Past values of y2 improve forecasts of y1 beyond y1's own past","y1 and y2 must be contemporaneously uncorrelated","y1 cannot also Granger-cause y2"],"answer":1,"hint":"The name is misleading; it is fundamentally a statement about forecasting.","explain":"Granger causality only certifies predictive content of y2's history for y1; it does not establish structural causation, allows correlation, and is not exclusive (causality can run both ways)."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: forecast bands","prompt":"As the forecast horizon h grows, the width of a VAR's forecast interval typically…","choices":["shrinks toward zero","stays constant","widens and then levels off at the stationary (unconditional) variance","oscillates without bound"],"answer":2,"hint":"For a stable VAR, the forecast converges to the mean and the forecast-error variance converges to a finite limit.","explain":"Forecast-error variance accumulates with horizon but, because the VAR is stationary, it converges to the series' unconditional variance, so the bands widen and then flatten rather than growing forever."}]}]},"panel":{"id":"panel","title":"Panel Data: Fixed & Random Effects","modules":[{"id":"panel-heterogeneity","title":"1 · Panel data & unobserved heterogeneity","summary":"What panel data is, why repeated observations on the same entities help, and how an unobserved entity effect αᵢ creates correlation that biases naive regression.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"What makes data 'panel' data","html":"<h2>Panel data: many entities, observed over time</h2><p>A <strong>panel</strong> (or longitudinal) dataset follows the <em>same</em> units &mdash; people, firms, countries, schools &mdash; across <em>multiple</em> time periods. We index every observation by an <strong>entity</strong> i and a <strong>time</strong> t:</p><p class=\"katexish\">yᵢₜ = β₀ + β₁·xᵢₜ + αᵢ + εᵢₜ</p><p>Here <code>yᵢₜ</code> is the outcome for entity i at time t, and <code>xᵢₜ</code> is a regressor that can vary across both i and t. A <em>balanced</em> panel has every entity observed in every period: N entities &times; T periods = N·T rows.</p><h3>The new ingredient: αᵢ</h3><p>The term <strong>αᵢ</strong> is an <em>entity effect</em> &mdash; a quantity that is constant within an entity but differs across entities. Think of a firm's management quality, a person's innate ability, or a country's institutions. It is <strong>unobserved</strong>: we don't have a column for it, yet it shapes y.</p><ul><li><code>εᵢₜ</code> is ordinary idiosyncratic noise (varies over i and t).</li><li><code>αᵢ</code> is fixed for the entity &mdash; it is the same in every period t for that i.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Panel data's superpower: because we see each entity repeatedly, we can isolate and remove the time-invariant αᵢ even though we never measure it directly. Cross-sectional (one-shot) data cannot do this.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Why αᵢ is dangerous: unobserved heterogeneity","html":"<h2>Unobserved heterogeneity and omitted-variable bias</h2><p>Regression gives unbiased slopes only when the regressor is uncorrelated with everything left in the error term. In the panel model, the <em>composite error</em> is <code>αᵢ + εᵢₜ</code>. The problem appears when the unobserved αᵢ is <strong>correlated with xᵢₜ</strong>:</p><p class=\"katexish\">Cov(xᵢₜ , αᵢ) ≠ 0</p><p>Then αᵢ is just an <em>omitted variable</em> that happens to be bundled with x. A regression of y on x alone will mistakenly credit x with variation that is really due to αᵢ.</p><h3>A concrete story</h3><p>Suppose y = wages, x = years of schooling, and αᵢ = unobserved ability. High-ability people both earn more (αᵢ pushes y up) <em>and</em> stay in school longer (αᵢ correlates with x). A naive wage-on-schooling regression blends the true return to schooling with the ability premium, <strong>overstating</strong> β₁.</p><ul><li>If <code>Cov(x, α) &gt; 0</code>, the naive slope is biased <strong>upward</strong>.</li><li>If <code>Cov(x, α) &lt; 0</code>, it is biased <strong>downward</strong>.</li><li>If <code>Cov(x, α) = 0</code>, there is no bias from α (though it still inflates the variance of the error).</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> The whole fixed-vs-random-effects debate is a debate about one quantity: is αᵢ correlated with xᵢₜ? If yes, you need fixed effects.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Build a balanced panel and look at it","note":"We simulate N=40 entities over T=6 periods. The entity effect alpha is correlated with x (corr≈0.8), so x carries some of alpha's fingerprint. True slope = 2.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 40, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\ntime   = np.tile(np.arange(T), N)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)            # one entity effect per entity\n# x is correlated with the entity's alpha:\nx = 0.8*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)   # true beta1 = 2\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'time': time, 'x': x, 'y': y})\nprint('panel shape (rows, cols):', df.shape, ' = N*T =', N*T)\nprint(df.head(8).to_string(index=False))\nprint('\\nCorr(x, alpha_i) =', round(np.corrcoef(x, alpha[entity])[0,1], 3))\n# colour each entity's cloud so the vertical shifts (the alpha_i) are visible\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nfor i in range(0, N, 8):\n    m = df['entity']==i\n    plt.scatter(df.x[m], df.y[m], s=18, label=f'entity {i}')\nplt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('y'); plt.title('each entity = a shifted cloud (its own alpha_i)')\nplt.legend(fontsize=7)"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: what is αᵢ?","prompt":"In yᵢₜ = β₀ + β₁·xᵢₜ + αᵢ + εᵢₜ, what distinguishes αᵢ from εᵢₜ?","choices":["αᵢ varies over time within an entity; εᵢₜ is constant","αᵢ is constant within an entity but differs across entities; εᵢₜ varies over both i and t","αᵢ is observed in the data; εᵢₜ is unobserved","They are identical; the notation is redundant"],"answer":1,"hint":"The 'i' subscript with no 't' is the giveaway.","explain":"αᵢ carries only an i subscript, so it is fixed across all periods of entity i but differs from entity to entity. εᵢₜ carries both subscripts and is the usual time-varying noise. Both are unobserved."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: when does αᵢ cause bias?","prompt":"A naive regression of y on x (ignoring αᵢ) gives a biased β₁ precisely when:","choices":["αᵢ has large variance","T is small","Cov(xᵢₜ, αᵢ) ≠ 0","εᵢₜ is heteroskedastic"],"answer":2,"hint":"Bias comes from correlation between the regressor and the omitted term.","explain":"Bias arises from omitted-variable correlation: if x is correlated with the omitted αᵢ, the slope absorbs αᵢ's effect. A large but uncorrelated αᵢ only inflates variance, it does not bias the slope."}]},{"id":"panel-pooled-bias","title":"2 · Pooled OLS and its bias","summary":"Pooled OLS ignores the panel structure and treats αᵢ as part of the error. We show, in code and with a slider, that this biases β₁ whenever αᵢ correlates with x.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Pooled OLS: pretend the panel is one big cross-section","html":"<h2>Pooled OLS</h2><p>The laziest estimator stacks all N·T rows and runs ordinary OLS as if every row were an independent draw:</p><p class=\"katexish\">yᵢₜ = β₀ + β₁·xᵢₜ + uᵢₜ ,  where uᵢₜ = αᵢ + εᵢₜ</p><p>This is just <code>smf.ols(\"y ~ x\", df)</code>. It throws the entity effect into the error term <code>uᵢₜ</code> and hopes for the best.</p><h3>When is pooled OLS okay?</h3><p>Pooled OLS is <em>consistent</em> only if the regressor is uncorrelated with the <strong>whole</strong> composite error, which requires <code>Cov(xᵢₜ, αᵢ) = 0</code>. If αᵢ is correlated with x, pooled OLS inherits exactly the omitted-variable bias from Module 1.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Pooled OLS = OLS that buries αᵢ in the error. It is unbiased when αᵢ ⟂ x, and biased otherwise. There is also a second, milder problem even when it's unbiased &mdash; read on.</div><h3>A second problem: clustered errors</h3><p>Even if <code>Cov(x, α)=0</code>, every observation of entity i shares the same αᵢ. That makes the errors <strong>correlated within an entity</strong> (serial / cluster correlation), so the default OLS standard errors are wrong &mdash; usually too small. Practitioners cluster standard errors by entity. We focus on the slope bias here, but keep this in mind.</p>"},{"type":"code","title":"Pooled OLS overshoots the true slope","note":"Same panel as Module 1 (true β₁ = 2, Corr(x, α) ≈ 0.8). Watch pooled OLS report a slope well above 2.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.formula.api as smf, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 40, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = 0.8*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)   # TRUE beta1 = 2\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\npooled = smf.ols('y ~ x', df).fit()\nprint('TRUE beta1        = 2.000')\nprint('Pooled OLS beta1  = %.3f' % pooled.params['x'], '  <-- biased upward')\nprint('Pooled intercept  = %.3f' % pooled.params['Intercept'])\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.scatter(df.x, df.y, s=10, color='#c9c6ac', alpha=.6)\nxs = np.linspace(df.x.min(), df.x.max(), 50)\nplt.plot(xs, pooled.params['Intercept']+pooled.params['x']*xs, color='#da9100', lw=2.5, label='pooled fit')\nplt.plot(xs, 1 + 2.0*xs, color='#6f6b57', lw=2, ls='--', label='true line (slope 2)')\nplt.legend(); plt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('y')\nplt.title('pooled beta1 = %.2f  vs  true 2.00' % pooled.params['x'])"},{"type":"read","title":"Why the pooled line tilts too steeply","html":"<h2>Seeing the bias geometrically</h2><p>Recall each entity is a little cloud sitting at its own height αᵢ. Because <code>x</code> is correlated with αᵢ, entities with <em>high x</em> are also the entities with <em>high αᵢ</em> &mdash; their whole cloud sits higher up. The pooled regression line tries to pass through all clouds at once, so it tilts <strong>steeper</strong> than the true within-cloud relationship to reach those raised high-x clouds.</p><ul><li>The <em>between-entity</em> variation (high-α clouds at high x) inflates the apparent slope.</li><li>The <em>within-entity</em> relationship &mdash; how y moves when x moves <em>for a fixed entity</em> &mdash; is the unbiased β₁ we actually want.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Pooled OLS mixes the between-entity and within-entity signals. When αᵢ correlates with x, the between part is contaminated, so the mix is biased. Fixed effects (Module 3) will throw away the between part and keep only the clean within part.</div>"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Slider: dial the αᵢ–x correlation","note":"Move the slider to change how strongly the entity effect loads onto x. The pooled slope is shown in the title; the true slope is always 2. At loading 0 pooled is fine; crank it up and pooled drifts away.","params":[{"name":"corr","label":"loading of α onto x","min":-1.5,"max":1.5,"step":0.1,"value":0.8}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.formula.api as smf, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 30, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = {{corr}}*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\npooled = smf.ols('y ~ x', df).fit()\nb = pooled.params['x']\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.scatter(df.x, df.y, s=10, color='#c9c6ac', alpha=.6)\nxs = np.linspace(df.x.min(), df.x.max(), 50)\nplt.plot(xs, pooled.params['Intercept']+b*xs, color='#da9100', lw=2.5, label='pooled')\nplt.plot(xs, 1+2.0*xs, color='#6f6b57', lw=2, ls='--', label='true (2.0)')\nplt.legend(); plt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('y')\nplt.title('pooled beta1 = %.2f   (true = 2.00)' % b)"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: direction of the bias","prompt":"You set the slider so α loads NEGATIVELY onto x (loading ≈ −1.5). What happens to the pooled slope relative to the true value of 2?","choices":["It stays at 2 — the sign of the loading doesn't matter","It is biased upward (above 2)","It is biased downward (below 2)","It becomes exactly 0"],"answer":2,"hint":"Bias direction follows the sign of Cov(x, α).","explain":"A negative loading makes Cov(x, α) < 0, so high-x entities sit lower (low α). That flattens the apparent slope, pulling pooled OLS below the true value of 2."}]},{"id":"panel-fixed-effects","title":"3 · Fixed effects: within transformation & entity dummies","summary":"Fixed effects estimate β₁ from within-entity variation only, sweeping out αᵢ. We do it two equivalent ways — entity dummies and hand-coded demeaning — and confirm both kill the bias.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"The within transformation: subtract each entity's mean","html":"<h2>Fixed effects = use only within-entity variation</h2><p>Start from the model and average it <em>over time within each entity</em> (the entity mean, denoted with a bar):</p><p class=\"katexish\">ȳᵢ = β₀ + β₁·x̄ᵢ + αᵢ + ε̄ᵢ</p><p>Now subtract this entity-mean equation from the original. The constant β₀ cancels, and crucially <strong>αᵢ − αᵢ = 0</strong> &mdash; the troublesome entity effect <em>vanishes</em>:</p><p class=\"katexish\">(yᵢₜ − ȳᵢ) = β₁·(xᵢₜ − x̄ᵢ) + (εᵢₜ − ε̄ᵢ)</p><p>This is the <strong>within (fixed-effects) transformation</strong>. We regress demeaned y on demeaned x &mdash; with <em>no intercept</em>, since demeaning already removed it. Because αᵢ is gone, any correlation between αᵢ and x can no longer bias β₁.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Demeaning within each entity removes <em>everything</em> that is constant within that entity &mdash; including the unobserved αᵢ. What remains is pure within-entity variation, which gives a consistent β₁ even when Cov(x, α) ≠ 0.</div><h3>The cost</h3><p>Anything that does not vary within an entity (gender, a country's geography, a time-invariant treatment) is also wiped out and <strong>cannot</strong> be estimated by FE. FE buys consistency for x by discarding between-entity information.</p>"},{"type":"code","title":"Method A — entity dummies","note":"Add a dummy for each entity with C(entity). This literally fits a separate intercept per entity, so x's slope uses only within-entity movement. Compare to the biased pooled slope.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 40, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = 0.8*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)   # true beta1 = 2\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\npooled = smf.ols('y ~ x', df).fit()\nfe_dum = smf.ols('y ~ x + C(entity)', df).fit()      # one intercept per entity\nprint('TRUE beta1            = 2.000')\nprint('Pooled OLS beta1      = %.3f  (biased)'    % pooled.params['x'])\nprint('FE (entity dummies)   = %.3f  (consistent)'% fe_dum.params['x'])\nprint('\\nFE estimated %d entity intercepts (the alpha_i absorbed).' % N)\nprint('FE std error on x     = %.3f' % fe_dum.bse['x'])"},{"type":"code","title":"Method B — demean by hand, then OLS","note":"groupby('entity').transform('mean') gives each row its entity mean. Subtract it from y and x, then OLS WITHOUT a constant. The slope matches the dummy approach exactly.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.api as sm, statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 40, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = 0.8*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\n# within transformation: subtract each entity's own mean\ndf['y_dm'] = df['y'] - df.groupby('entity')['y'].transform('mean')\ndf['x_dm'] = df['x'] - df.groupby('entity')['x'].transform('mean')\nwithin = sm.OLS(df['y_dm'], df['x_dm']).fit()   # NO constant: demeaning removed it\nfe_dum = smf.ols('y ~ x + C(entity)', df).fit()\nprint('Within (hand demean) beta1 = %.4f' % within.params.iloc[0])\nprint('FE (entity dummies)  beta1 = %.4f' % fe_dum.params['x'])\nprint('--> identical slope: the two methods are algebraically the same.')\nmsg = '\\nNote: the hand-demeaned SE = %.4f is slightly too SMALL because OLS' % within.bse.iloc[0]\nprint(msg)\nprint('did not subtract the N = %d degrees of freedom for the absorbed' % N)\nprint('entity intercepts. The dummy version SE = %.4f is the correct one.' % fe_dum.bse['x'])"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Slider: pooled drifts, fixed effects holds","note":"Same loading slider as Module 2, but now both estimators are shown. No matter how you move it, the FE slope stays pinned near the true value of 2 while pooled wanders.","params":[{"name":"corr","label":"loading of α onto x","min":-1.5,"max":1.5,"step":0.1,"value":0.9}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.api as sm, statsmodels.formula.api as smf, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 30, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = {{corr}}*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\npooled = smf.ols('y ~ x', df).fit().params['x']\nyd = df['y'] - df.groupby('entity')['y'].transform('mean')\nxd = df['x'] - df.groupby('entity')['x'].transform('mean')\nfe = sm.OLS(yd, xd).fit().params.iloc[0]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.axhline(2.0, color='#6f6b57', ls='--', lw=2, label='true = 2')\nplt.bar(['pooled','fixed effects'], [pooled, fe], color=['#da9100','#af983f'])\nplt.ylabel('estimated beta1'); plt.ylim(0, max(3.2, pooled+0.5))\nplt.legend()\nplt.title('pooled = %.2f   FE = %.2f   (true 2.00)' % (pooled, fe))"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: why no constant?","prompt":"After the within transformation (yᵢₜ − ȳᵢ) on (xᵢₜ − x̄ᵢ), why do we run OLS WITHOUT an intercept?","choices":["An intercept would reintroduce αᵢ","Demeaning forces both variables to have mean zero, so the intercept is identically zero","statsmodels does not allow intercepts in panel models","To save a degree of freedom for the slope"],"answer":1,"hint":"What is the mean of a demeaned variable?","explain":"Subtracting each entity's mean makes the overall mean of both demeaned variables exactly zero, so any fitted intercept would be zero. We drop it (and the dummy method already fits per-entity intercepts that play that role)."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: what FE cannot estimate","prompt":"Your panel includes a regressor 'female' that never changes within a person. What does the fixed-effects (within) estimator do with its coefficient?","choices":["Estimates it precisely","Cannot estimate it — demeaning wipes out any time-invariant regressor","Estimates it but with the wrong sign","Uses it as the entity effect"],"answer":1,"hint":"Demean a variable that is constant within an entity — what's left?","explain":"A within-entity-constant variable becomes all zeros after demeaning, so FE has no variation to estimate its coefficient. This is FE's main limitation; random effects (Module 4) can recover such coefficients if its assumption holds."}]},{"id":"panel-random-effects","title":"4 · Random effects & choosing FE vs RE","summary":"Random effects treats αᵢ as random noise uncorrelated with x, gaining efficiency and time-invariant coefficients — but only if that assumption holds. The Hausman idea tells you which to trust.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Random effects: αᵢ as a random draw","html":"<h2>The random-effects model</h2><p>Random effects (RE) keeps the same equation but makes a strong <strong>assumption</strong> about αᵢ:</p><p class=\"katexish\">yᵢₜ = β₀ + β₁·xᵢₜ + αᵢ + εᵢₜ ,  with  Cov(xᵢₜ, αᵢ) = 0</p><p>RE treats αᵢ as a <em>random</em> entity-specific intercept drawn from a distribution with variance σ²_α, <strong>uncorrelated with x</strong>. Estimation is by (feasible) GLS, which is equivalent to a partial demeaning: subtract a fraction θ of each entity mean, where</p><p class=\"katexish\">θ = 1 − √( σ²_ε / (σ²_ε + T·σ²_α) )</p><h3>FE vs RE as two extremes of partial demeaning</h3><ul><li><strong>Pooled OLS</strong> subtracts θ = 0 of the entity mean (no demeaning).</li><li><strong>Fixed effects</strong> subtracts θ = 1 (full demeaning).</li><li><strong>Random effects</strong> picks θ between 0 and 1 &mdash; a data-driven compromise.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> If Cov(x, α) = 0, RE is the <em>best</em> estimator: it is consistent like FE but more <strong>efficient</strong> (smaller standard errors), and it can estimate time-invariant regressors. If Cov(x, α) ≠ 0, RE is <strong>biased</strong> &mdash; only FE survives.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Random effects via MixedLM (αᵢ ⟂ x)","note":"When the entity effect is genuinely uncorrelated with x, RE and FE agree on the slope, but RE reports a smaller standard error. We fit a random-intercept model with mixedlm and compare to the correctly-counted FE standard error (the dummy version).","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 50, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)                 # loading = 0: alpha independent of x\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\n# Fixed effects via entity dummies (gives the CORRECT standard error)\nfe = smf.ols('y ~ x + C(entity)', df).fit()\n# Random effects: random intercept per entity\nre = smf.mixedlm('y ~ x', df, groups=df['entity']).fit()\nprint('TRUE beta1 = 2.000   (here Cov(x, alpha) = 0)')\nprint('FE  beta1 = %.3f   SE = %.3f' % (fe.params['x'], fe.bse['x']))\nprint('RE  beta1 = %.3f   SE = %.3f' % (re.params['x'], re.bse['x']))\nprint('\\nEstimated entity-effect variance (Group Var) = %.3f' % re.cov_re.iloc[0,0])\nprint('--> slopes agree; RE has the smaller SE, so it is more efficient here.')"},{"type":"code","title":"Now break RE's assumption: αᵢ correlates with x","note":"Reintroduce correlation between alpha and x. RE (like pooled) is now biased; only FE recovers 2. This divergence is exactly what a Hausman test detects.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.api as sm, statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 50, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = 0.9*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)   # loading != 0: RE assumption FAILS\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\nyd = df['y'] - df.groupby('entity')['y'].transform('mean')\nxd = df['x'] - df.groupby('entity')['x'].transform('mean')\nfe = sm.OLS(yd, xd).fit().params.iloc[0]\nre = smf.mixedlm('y ~ x', df, groups=df['entity']).fit().params['x']\nprint('TRUE beta1 = 2.000   (Cov(x, alpha) != 0)')\nprint('FE  beta1 = %.3f   <- still consistent' % fe)\nprint('RE  beta1 = %.3f   <- biased away from 2' % re)\nprint('\\nThe gap |FE - RE| = %.3f signals RE is inconsistent here.' % abs(fe-re))\nprint('A large gap => prefer FE (this is the Hausman intuition).')"},{"type":"read","title":"The Hausman idea: let the gap decide","html":"<h2>Choosing FE vs RE: the Hausman intuition</h2><p>The two estimators react differently to a correlated αᵢ:</p><ul><li><strong>FE</strong> is consistent <em>whether or not</em> Cov(x, α) = 0 &mdash; it always demeans αᵢ away. Its price is lost efficiency and no time-invariant coefficients.</li><li><strong>RE</strong> is consistent <em>and</em> efficient <em>only if</em> Cov(x, α) = 0. If that fails, RE is biased.</li></ul><p>So compare them. Under the RE assumption both estimate the same β₁, so <code>β̂_FE − β̂_RE</code> should be small (just sampling noise). If the assumption fails, RE is pulled away while FE stays put, and the gap grows.</p><p class=\"katexish\">H = (β̂_FE − β̂_RE)′ · [Var(β̂_FE) − Var(β̂_RE)]⁻¹ · (β̂_FE − β̂_RE)</p><p>This <strong>Hausman statistic</strong> is large when the estimators disagree. A large H (small p-value) rejects RE &rarr; use <strong>fixed effects</strong>. A small H means RE and FE agree, so you may keep the more efficient <strong>random effects</strong>.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Decision rule.</b> Estimators disagree (large Hausman) → trust FE. Estimators agree (small Hausman) → RE is safe and more efficient. When in doubt, or when α plausibly correlates with x (the usual case in economics), default to FE.</div><h3>Practical checklist</h3><ul><li>Need a coefficient on a time-invariant variable? RE (or pooled) is your only option &mdash; FE cannot give it.</li><li>Worried about unobserved confounders correlated with x? FE is the safe, robust choice.</li><li>Either way, cluster standard errors by entity to handle within-entity correlation.</li></ul>"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Slider: watch FE and RE diverge","note":"As the α–x loading grows, RE (and pooled) drift from the true 2, while FE stays put. The growing FE–RE gap is the engine of the Hausman test.","params":[{"name":"corr","label":"loading of α onto x","min":0,"max":1.5,"step":0.1,"value":0}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.api as sm, statsmodels.formula.api as smf, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nN, T = 40, 6\nentity = np.repeat(np.arange(N), T)\nalpha  = rng.normal(0, 2, N)\nx = {{corr}}*alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ny = 1 + 2.0*x + alpha[entity] + rng.normal(0, 1, N*T)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'entity': entity, 'x': x, 'y': y})\npooled = smf.ols('y ~ x', df).fit().params['x']\nyd = df['y'] - df.groupby('entity')['y'].transform('mean')\nxd = df['x'] - df.groupby('entity')['x'].transform('mean')\nfe = sm.OLS(yd, xd).fit().params.iloc[0]\nre = smf.mixedlm('y ~ x', df, groups=df['entity']).fit().params['x']\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.axhline(2.0, color='#6f6b57', ls='--', lw=2, label='true = 2')\nplt.bar(['pooled','RE','FE'], [pooled, re, fe], color=['#c9c6ac','#da9100','#af983f'])\nplt.ylabel('estimated beta1'); plt.ylim(0, max(3.4, pooled+0.5)); plt.legend()\nplt.title('FE=%.2f  RE=%.2f  gap=%.2f' % (fe, re, abs(fe-re)))"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: reading the Hausman result","prompt":"A Hausman test returns a large statistic (p-value ≈ 0.001), meaning FE and RE estimates differ sharply. What should you conclude?","choices":["RE is fine and more efficient — keep RE","Reject RE; αᵢ is correlated with x, so use fixed effects","Both estimators are biased; use pooled OLS","Increase T and re-run RE"],"answer":1,"hint":"A big gap means RE's zero-correlation assumption is failing.","explain":"A large Hausman statistic signals the estimators disagree, which happens when Cov(x, α) ≠ 0 and RE is inconsistent. You reject RE and rely on the always-consistent fixed-effects estimator."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: when RE wins","prompt":"You need the effect of a time-invariant regressor (e.g. a person's birth region) AND a Hausman test does not reject RE. Which estimator do you use?","choices":["Fixed effects — it is always safest","Random effects — it is consistent here and, unlike FE, can estimate time-invariant regressors","Pooled OLS — it ignores αᵢ","Neither is identified"],"answer":1,"hint":"FE demeans away time-invariant variables; RE does not.","explain":"When Hausman does not reject (αᵢ ⟂ x), RE is consistent and efficient, and crucially it retains time-invariant regressors that FE would wipe out — so RE is the right choice."}]}]},"logit":{"id":"logit","title":"Logit & Probit (Binary Outcomes)","modules":[{"id":"logit-why-lpm-fails","title":"1 · Why a linear model fails for 0/1 outcomes","summary":"When y is 0 or 1, fitting a straight line (the Linear Probability Model) breaks down: it predicts probabilities outside [0,1], has built-in heteroskedasticity, and imposes a constant effect that cannot be globally true.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Binary outcomes and the urge to run OLS","html":"<h2>Modelling a yes/no outcome</h2><p>Many outcomes are binary: a loan <em>defaults</em> or not, a patient <em>recovers</em> or not, a customer <em>buys</em> or not. We code these as <code>y ∈ {0,1}</code>. The natural quantity of interest is the <strong>probability</strong> that <code>y = 1</code> given some features x:</p><p class=\"katexish\">p(x) = P(y = 1 | x)</p><p>A tempting first move is to just run OLS of y on x. That is called the <strong>Linear Probability Model (LPM)</strong>:</p><p class=\"katexish\">P(y = 1 | x) = β₀ + β₁·x₁ + β₂·x₂ + …</p><p>It is fast, the coefficients read directly as \"a one-unit rise in x changes the probability by β\", and for x near the data centre it is often a decent approximation. But it has three structural problems that get worse near the edges.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> A probability must live in [0,1] and the way x moves it cannot be a straight line forever — eventually the line must bend, or it will predict nonsense.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Three things that go wrong","html":"<h3>1. Predictions escape [0,1]</h3><p>A line has no ceiling or floor. Push x far enough and ŷ goes below 0 or above 1 — an <em>impossible</em> probability. You cannot interpret \"there is a 130% chance of default.\"</p><h3>2. The errors are heteroskedastic by construction</h3><p>If y is Bernoulli with success probability p, its variance is <code>p·(1−p)</code>. That depends on x, so the OLS homoskedasticity assumption is automatically violated and the default standard errors are wrong.</p><h3>3. A constant marginal effect is implausible</h3><p>LPM says one extra unit of x always changes the probability by the same β. But moving someone from a 50% chance to 60% is easy, while moving them from 98% to 100% should be nearly impossible. The real effect of x must <em>shrink near 0 and 1</em>.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> We want an S-shaped curve that is squeezed into (0,1): steep in the middle, flat at the extremes. That is exactly what logit and probit deliver.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Watch the LPM predict impossible probabilities","note":"We simulate a true logistic process, fit OLS (the LPM), and print how many fitted 'probabilities' fall outside [0,1]. The scatter shows the flat line cutting through 0 and 1.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 600\nx = rng.normal(0, 1.6, n)\neta = -0.4 + 2.2*x            # true log-odds\np = 1/(1+np.exp(-eta))        # true probability\ny = rng.binomial(1, p)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'y': y, 'x': x})\n\nlpm = smf.ols('y ~ x', df).fit()\nphat = lpm.predict(df)\nout = ((phat < 0) | (phat > 1)).sum()\nprint('LPM fitted prob range: [%.3f, %.3f]' % (phat.min(), phat.max()))\nprint('Fitted values outside [0,1]: %d of %d (%.1f%%)' % (out, n, 100*out/n))\n\nxs = np.linspace(x.min(), x.max(), 100)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.scatter(x, y, s=10, color='#da9100', alpha=.35, label='data (0/1)')\nplt.plot(xs, lpm.params['Intercept']+lpm.params['x']*xs, color='#6f6b57', lw=2, label='LPM line')\nplt.axhline(0, color='#c9c6ac', lw=1); plt.axhline(1, color='#c9c6ac', lw=1)\nplt.ylim(-0.4, 1.4); plt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('y / predicted prob')\nplt.title('LPM: %d impossible predictions outside [0,1]' % out)\nplt.legend(loc='best', fontsize=8)"},{"type":"interactive","title":"How far out of bounds does the line go?","note":"Increase the true slope or shift the intercept. The steeper and more off-centre the relationship, the further the straight LPM line pokes past 0 and 1. The title reports how many fitted probabilities are impossible.","params":[{"name":"slope","label":"True log-odds slope","min":0.2,"max":5,"step":0.1,"value":2.2},{"name":"intercept","label":"True log-odds intercept","min":-3,"max":3,"step":0.1,"value":-0.4}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd, statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 500\nx = rng.normal(0, 1.6, n)\neta = {{intercept}} + {{slope}}*x\np = 1/(1+np.exp(-eta))\ny = rng.binomial(1, p)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'y': y, 'x': x})\nlpm = smf.ols('y ~ x', df).fit()\nphat = lpm.predict(df)\nout = int(((phat < 0) | (phat > 1)).sum())\nxs = np.linspace(x.min(), x.max(), 100)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.scatter(x, y, s=10, color='#da9100', alpha=.3)\nplt.plot(xs, lpm.params['Intercept']+lpm.params['x']*xs, color='#6f6b57', lw=2)\nplt.axhline(0, color='#c9c6ac', lw=1); plt.axhline(1, color='#c9c6ac', lw=1)\nplt.ylim(-0.5, 1.5); plt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('predicted prob')\nplt.title('LPM impossible predictions: %d of %d' % (out, n))"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"Which problem is NOT a structural flaw of the Linear Probability Model for a 0/1 outcome?","choices":["Fitted probabilities can fall below 0 or above 1","The error variance p(1−p) depends on x, so errors are heteroskedastic","It forces a constant marginal effect of x at every probability level","It can only be estimated by maximum likelihood, never by OLS"],"answer":3,"hint":"Think about how the LPM is actually fit versus what logit/probit require.","explain":"The LPM is fit by ordinary least squares — that is its whole appeal. The genuine flaws are out-of-range predictions, built-in heteroskedasticity, and a constant (rather than diminishing) marginal effect."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"Why should the effect of x on P(y=1) shrink as the probability approaches 0 or 1?","choices":["Because the sample size falls at the extremes","Because a probability is bounded in [0,1], so there is little room left to move","Because OLS standard errors grow at the extremes","Because x stops varying at the extremes"],"answer":1,"hint":"You cannot push a probability above 1.","explain":"Near the floor or ceiling there is almost no room left, so the same change in x can only nudge the probability a tiny amount — implying an S-shaped curve, not a straight line."}]},{"id":"logit-the-model","title":"2 · The logit (and probit) model","summary":"Logit and probit pass a linear index β₀+β₁x through an S-shaped link (the logistic CDF or the standard-normal CDF) to keep probabilities in (0,1). Both are fit by maximum likelihood; their coefficients differ only by a roughly constant scale factor.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Squash the line through an S-curve","html":"<h2>From a line to a probability</h2><p>Keep the familiar linear part — call it the <strong>linear index</strong> or <em>log-odds</em>:</p><p class=\"katexish\">η = β₀ + β₁·x₁ + β₂·x₂ + …</p><p>η ranges over the whole real line, from −∞ to +∞. To turn it into a probability we feed it through a smooth, increasing function that maps (−∞, +∞) into (0,1). For <strong>logit</strong> that function is the <em>logistic</em> (a.k.a. sigmoid):</p><p class=\"katexish\">p = Λ(η) = 1 / (1 + e^(−η))</p><p>For <strong>probit</strong> it is the standard-normal CDF Φ:</p><p class=\"katexish\">p = Φ(η)</p><p>Both are S-shaped: near η = 0 they pass through p = 0.5 and rise steeply; as η → ±∞ they flatten against 1 and 0. The probability can never escape the unit interval — problem #1 from Module 1 is solved automatically.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Same linear index, different S-shaped link. Logit uses the logistic CDF; probit uses the normal CDF.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"The logit's special trick: log-odds are linear","html":"<h3>Odds and log-odds</h3><p>The <em>odds</em> of success are <code>p / (1 − p)</code> — e.g. p = 0.8 means odds of 4-to-1. Invert the logistic and something elegant pops out:</p><p class=\"katexish\">log( p / (1 − p) ) = β₀ + β₁·x₁ + …</p><p>So in the logit model the <strong>log-odds are exactly linear</strong> in x. That is why β₁ has a clean meaning: a one-unit rise in x₁ adds β₁ to the log-odds, i.e. multiplies the odds by <code>e^(β₁)</code>. (We exploit this in Module 3.)</p><h3>How are they fit?</h3><p>There is no closed-form least-squares solution. Instead we choose the β's that make the observed 0/1 pattern most probable — <strong>maximum likelihood (MLE)</strong>. statsmodels does this for you: <code>smf.logit(...).fit()</code> and <code>smf.probit(...).fit()</code>. The output reports z-statistics (not t), a Log-Likelihood, and a <em>Pseudo R²</em> instead of the usual R².</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Logit makes the <em>log-odds</em> linear in x; probit makes a <em>normal z-score</em> linear in x. Both are estimated by maximum likelihood.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Fit logit, recover the truth, and compare to probit","note":"We simulate from a known logistic process (intercept −0.3, slopes +1.5 and −0.8), fit logit by MLE, and confirm the estimates land near the truth. Then we fit probit on the same data and show the coefficients differ by a near-constant scale (~1.6–1.8), even though the fitted probabilities almost coincide.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\nx1 = rng.normal(0, 1, n); x2 = rng.normal(0, 1, n)\neta = -0.3 + 1.5*x1 - 0.8*x2          # true log-odds\np = 1/(1+np.exp(-eta))\ny = rng.binomial(1, p)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'y': y, 'x1': x1, 'x2': x2})\n\nlogit = smf.logit('y ~ x1 + x2', df).fit(disp=0)\nprobit = smf.probit('y ~ x1 + x2', df).fit(disp=0)\nprint(logit.summary())\nprint('\\nTrue coefs:      const=-0.30  x1= 1.50  x2=-0.80')\nprint('Logit  estimates:', np.round(logit.params.values, 3))\nprint('Probit estimates:', np.round(probit.params.values, 3))\nprint('Logit/Probit ratio (x1, x2):',\n      np.round((logit.params[['x1','x2']]/probit.params[['x1','x2']]).values, 3))\nph_l = logit.predict(df); ph_p = probit.predict(df)\nprint('Max abs gap between logit & probit fitted probs: %.4f' % (np.abs(ph_l-ph_p).max()))"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Shape the S-curve by hand","note":"Move the index intercept β₀ and slope β₁ and watch the logistic curve P(y=1)=1/(1+e^-(β₀+β₁x)). The curve crosses 0.5 where the index is zero (x = −β₀/β₁), and its steepest slope equals β₁/4. Bigger |β₁| = sharper S.","params":[{"name":"b0","label":"β₀ (index intercept)","min":-4,"max":4,"step":0.1,"value":-0.5},{"name":"b1","label":"β₁ (index slope)","min":-4,"max":4,"step":0.1,"value":1.5}],"template":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy.stats import norm\nxs = np.linspace(-6, 6, 300)\nlog_p = 1/(1+np.exp(-({{b0}}+{{b1}}*xs)))\nprob_p = norm.cdf({{b0}}+{{b1}}*xs)\nb1 = {{b1}}\nx50 = (-{{b0}}/b1) if abs(b1) > 1e-9 else float('nan')\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(xs, log_p, color='#da9100', lw=2.4, label='logit (logistic)')\nplt.plot(xs, prob_p, color='#af983f', lw=1.6, ls='--', label='probit (normal)')\nplt.axhline(0.5, color='#6f6b57', lw=1, ls=':')\nplt.ylim(-0.03, 1.03); plt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('P(y=1)')\nplt.title('crosses 0.5 at x=%.2f   max slope (logit)=%.3f' % (x50, b1/4))\nplt.legend(loc='lower right', fontsize=8)"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"In a logit model, a one-unit increase in x₁ (holding others fixed) does exactly what?","choices":["Adds β₁ to the probability P(y=1)","Adds β₁ to the log-odds of y=1","Multiplies the probability by β₁","Adds β₁ to the normal z-score Φ⁻¹(p)"],"answer":1,"hint":"What is the inverse of the logistic link?","explain":"The logit link makes log[p/(1−p)] = β₀+β₁x linear, so a unit rise in x₁ adds exactly β₁ to the log-odds (adding β₁ to the normal z-score is the probit statement, not logit)."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"You fit the same data with logit and probit. What do you expect?","choices":["Identical coefficients and identical fitted probabilities","Very different fitted probabilities but identical coefficients","Coefficients differing by a roughly constant scale, with nearly identical fitted probabilities","Probit coefficients exactly double the logit coefficients"],"answer":2,"hint":"The logistic and normal CDFs have different variances but similar S-shapes.","explain":"Because the logistic and normal links have different scales, logit coefficients run about 1.6–1.8 times the probit ones, yet the predicted probabilities and overall fit are almost indistinguishable."}]},{"id":"logit-interpreting-coefficients","title":"3 · Interpreting coefficients: odds ratios & marginal effects","summary":"A raw logit coefficient is a change in log-odds — not very intuitive. Exponentiate it to get an odds ratio (a multiplicative effect on the odds), or compute a marginal effect dP/dx (the change in probability), which is largest near p=0.5 and shrinks at the extremes.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Three currencies for the same effect","html":"<h2>Log-odds, odds, or probability?</h2><p>The estimated β₁ is in <strong>log-odds units</strong>, which almost nobody thinks in. We translate it into two friendlier currencies.</p><h3>Odds ratio: e^(β₁)</h3><p>Because the log-odds are linear, exponentiating undoes the log:</p><p class=\"katexish\">odds-ratio = e^(β₁)</p><p>A one-unit rise in x₁ <em>multiplies</em> the odds by e^(β₁). If β₁ = 0.7 then e^(0.7) ≈ 2.0: the odds double. If e^(β₁) > 1 the feature raises the odds; if < 1 it lowers them; if = 1 it has no effect. This factor is <strong>constant</strong> everywhere — that is the elegance of the odds scale.</p><h3>Marginal effect: dP/dx</h3><p>People often want the effect on the <em>probability</em>, not the odds. For logit:</p><p class=\"katexish\">∂P/∂x₁ = β₁ · p · (1 − p)</p><p>This is <strong>not constant</strong> — it depends on where you are on the curve. It peaks at p = 0.5 (factor 0.25·β₁) and vanishes as p → 0 or 1. statsmodels averages it across the sample with <code>res.get_margeff()</code> (the <em>Average Marginal Effect</em>).</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Odds ratio = constant multiplicative effect on the odds. Marginal effect = change in probability, biggest in the middle of the S, smallest at the tails.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Reading the numbers correctly","html":"<h3>Pitfalls to avoid</h3><ul><li><strong>Do not</strong> read the raw β as a probability change — it is a log-odds change.</li><li>An odds ratio of 2.0 does <em>not</em> mean the probability doubles. Going from 50% (odds 1) to odds 2 is 67%, not 100%.</li><li>The marginal effect needs a reference point. <em>Average Marginal Effect (AME)</em> computes dP/dx for every observation and averages — usually the most honest summary. <em>Marginal Effect at the Means (MEM)</em> plugs in the average x instead.</li><li>For a <strong>binary</strong> regressor, the cleaner summary is the discrete change in predicted probability from 0 to 1, not the derivative.</li></ul><p>Rule of thumb for logit: divide a coefficient by 4 to get the <em>maximum</em> marginal effect on probability. β₁ = 1.5 → up to about 0.375 change in probability per unit of x, near p = 0.5.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> The 'divide-by-4' rule gives the steepest possible probability effect; the actual average marginal effect is usually smaller because most observations sit off the p=0.5 peak.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Odds ratios and average marginal effects","note":"We fit logit, exponentiate the coefficients to read odds ratios, and call get_margeff() for the average marginal effects (the change in probability). Notice the AME for x1 is well below β1/4 — most observations are off the steepest part of the curve.","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\nx1 = rng.normal(0, 1, n); x2 = rng.normal(0, 1, n)\neta = -0.3 + 1.5*x1 - 0.8*x2\np = 1/(1+np.exp(-eta)); y = rng.binomial(1, p)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'y': y, 'x1': x1, 'x2': x2})\n\nres = smf.logit('y ~ x1 + x2', df).fit(disp=0)\nodds = np.exp(res.params)\nprint('Coefficients (log-odds):')\nprint(np.round(res.params, 3).to_string())\nprint('\\nOdds ratios  exp(coef):')\nprint(np.round(odds, 3).to_string())\nprint('\\nInterpretation: +1 in x1 multiplies the odds by %.2f' % odds['x1'])\nprint('               +1 in x2 multiplies the odds by %.2f (i.e. lowers them)' % odds['x2'])\nprint('\\ndivide-by-4 max marginal effect for x1: %.3f' % (res.params['x1']/4))\nprint('\\n', res.get_margeff().summary())"},{"type":"interactive","title":"See the marginal effect change along the curve","note":"Pick a slope β₁ and a point x*. The orange dot is P(y=1) there; the dashed tangent is the local slope dP/dx = β₁·p·(1−p). Drag x* toward the tails and watch the tangent flatten even though β₁ never changes — that is why 'the' marginal effect is not a single number.","params":[{"name":"b1","label":"β₁ (log-odds slope)","min":0.2,"max":4,"step":0.1,"value":1.5},{"name":"xstar","label":"x* (evaluation point)","min":-5,"max":5,"step":0.1,"value":0}],"template":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nb1 = {{b1}}; b0 = 0.0; xstar = {{xstar}}\nxs = np.linspace(-6, 6, 300)\nP = 1/(1+np.exp(-(b0+b1*xs)))\nps = 1/(1+np.exp(-(b0+b1*xstar)))\nslope = b1*ps*(1-ps)            # dP/dx at x*\ntang = ps + slope*(xs - xstar)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(xs, P, color='#da9100', lw=2.4)\nplt.plot(xs, np.clip(tang,-0.1,1.1), color='#6f6b57', lw=1.5, ls='--')\nplt.scatter([xstar],[ps], color='#af983f', zorder=5, s=40)\nplt.ylim(-0.03,1.03); plt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('P(y=1)')\nplt.title('at x*=%.1f:  P=%.3f   dP/dx=%.3f   (max=%.3f)' % (xstar, ps, slope, b1/4))"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"A logit gives a coefficient of 0.69 on x₁. What is the cleanest one-line interpretation?","choices":["A one-unit rise in x₁ raises P(y=1) by 0.69","A one-unit rise in x₁ roughly doubles the odds of y=1, since e^0.69≈2","A one-unit rise in x₁ doubles the probability of y=1","x₁ explains 69% of the variation in y"],"answer":1,"hint":"Exponentiate the coefficient.","explain":"e^0.69 ≈ 2, so the odds (not the probability) are multiplied by about 2 — the odds ratio is the constant, exponentiated read of a logit coefficient."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"Why is the average marginal effect (dP/dx) usually smaller than coefficient/4?","choices":["Because statsmodels uses robust standard errors","Because coefficient/4 is the maximum effect, reached only at p=0.5, and most observations sit away from there","Because marginal effects ignore the intercept","Because the AME is measured in odds, not probability"],"answer":1,"hint":"Where on the S-curve is p·(1−p) largest?","explain":"The factor p·(1−p) peaks at 0.25 when p=0.5, giving the divide-by-4 maximum; observations in the flatter tails contribute smaller slopes, pulling the average below that peak."}]},{"id":"logit-fit-and-classification","title":"4 · Model fit & classification","summary":"Judge a binary model by likelihood-based fit (pseudo-R², LR test) and by classification: turn predicted probabilities into 0/1 at a threshold, build a confusion matrix, read accuracy, and trace the threshold-free ROC curve and its AUC — all with numpy, no sklearn.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"Two ways to ask 'is it any good?'","html":"<h2>Fit versus classification</h2><p>There is no ordinary R² in MLE land. statsmodels reports <strong>McFadden's Pseudo R²</strong> = 1 − ℓ(model)/ℓ(null), comparing the fitted log-likelihood to a null model with only an intercept. Values of 0.2–0.4 already indicate a strong fit — do not expect the 0.9 you might see in OLS. The <strong>LLR p-value</strong> is a likelihood-ratio test that all slopes are jointly zero (the binary analogue of the overall F-test).</p><h3>From probabilities to decisions</h3><p>To actually <em>classify</em>, pick a threshold (commonly 0.5) and predict ŷ = 1 when p̂ ≥ threshold. Cross-tabulate against the truth to get the <strong>confusion matrix</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>TP</strong> true positives, <strong>TN</strong> true negatives — correct calls.</li><li><strong>FP</strong> false positives (predicted 1, actually 0), <strong>FN</strong> false negatives (predicted 0, actually 1) — the two error types.</li></ul><p class=\"katexish\">accuracy = (TP + TN) / n</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Pseudo-R² and the LR test grade the <em>probabilities</em>; the confusion matrix and accuracy grade the <em>0/1 decisions</em> at a chosen threshold.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Why one threshold is not enough: ROC & AUC","html":"<h3>Accuracy can lie</h3><p>If 90% of cases are y = 0, a lazy model that always predicts 0 scores 90% accuracy while catching zero positives. With imbalance you need to look at both error types and to vary the threshold.</p><h3>The ROC curve</h3><p>Sweep the threshold from 1 down to 0. At each one record the <strong>true-positive rate</strong> TPR = TP/(all actual 1s) and the <strong>false-positive rate</strong> FPR = FP/(all actual 0s). Plotting TPR against FPR traces the <strong>ROC curve</strong>. A perfect model hugs the top-left corner; random guessing is the 45° diagonal.</p><p>The area under that curve, the <strong>AUC</strong>, summarises ranking quality in one number: it equals the probability that a randomly chosen positive gets a higher p̂ than a randomly chosen negative. AUC = 0.5 is coin-flipping; AUC = 1.0 is perfect separation; 0.8+ is generally strong. AUC is <em>threshold-free</em>, so it judges the probabilities themselves rather than one cut-off.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> AUC measures how well the model <em>ranks</em> positives above negatives, independent of any threshold — robust even when classes are imbalanced.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Confusion matrix, accuracy, and a hand-rolled ROC/AUC","note":"Pure numpy: threshold predicted probabilities at 0.5 for the confusion matrix and accuracy, then sweep all thresholds to build the ROC curve and integrate it with the trapezoid rule (no sklearn, no np.trapz).","code":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\nx1 = rng.normal(0, 1, n); x2 = rng.normal(0, 1, n)\neta = -0.3 + 1.5*x1 - 0.8*x2\np = 1/(1+np.exp(-eta)); y = rng.binomial(1, p)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'y': y, 'x1': x1, 'x2': x2})\nres = smf.logit('y ~ x1 + x2', df).fit(disp=0)\n\ny = df['y'].to_numpy()\nphat = res.predict(df).to_numpy()\nyhat = (phat >= 0.5).astype(int)\nTP = int(((yhat==1)&(y==1)).sum()); TN = int(((yhat==0)&(y==0)).sum())\nFP = int(((yhat==1)&(y==0)).sum()); FN = int(((yhat==0)&(y==1)).sum())\nacc = (TP+TN)/n\nprint('Pseudo R-squared: %.3f' % res.prsquared)\nprint('Confusion matrix (rows=actual, cols=predicted):')\nprint('            pred 0   pred 1')\nprint('actual 0   %6d   %6d' % (TN, FP))\nprint('actual 1   %6d   %6d' % (FN, TP))\nprint('Accuracy @0.5: %.3f' % acc)\n\nP = (y==1).sum(); N = (y==0).sum()\nthr = np.linspace(0, 1, 201)\ntpr = np.array([((phat>=t)&(y==1)).sum()/P for t in thr])\nfpr = np.array([((phat>=t)&(y==0)).sum()/N for t in thr])\norder = np.argsort(fpr); fs = fpr[order]; ts = tpr[order]\nauc = float(np.sum((fs[1:]-fs[:-1])*(ts[1:]+ts[:-1])/2))   # trapezoid\nprint('AUC: %.3f' % auc)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(fpr, tpr, color='#da9100', lw=2.2, label='ROC (AUC=%.3f)' % auc)\nplt.plot([0,1],[0,1], color='#6f6b57', lw=1, ls='--', label='random')\nplt.xlabel('False positive rate'); plt.ylabel('True positive rate')\nplt.title('ROC curve   accuracy@0.5=%.3f' % acc)\nplt.legend(loc='lower right', fontsize=8)"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Move the threshold, trade off the two errors","note":"Slide the decision threshold. Raising it makes the model more conservative about predicting 1: false positives fall but false negatives rise. Accuracy is one summary of that trade-off — and its best value is rarely exactly at 0.5.","params":[{"name":"thresh","label":"Decision threshold","min":0.05,"max":0.95,"step":0.05,"value":0.5}],"template":"import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\nx1 = rng.normal(0, 1, n); x2 = rng.normal(0, 1, n)\neta = -0.3 + 1.5*x1 - 0.8*x2\np = 1/(1+np.exp(-eta)); y = rng.binomial(1, p)\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'y': y, 'x1': x1, 'x2': x2})\nres = smf.logit('y ~ x1 + x2', df).fit(disp=0)\ny = df['y'].to_numpy(); phat = res.predict(df).to_numpy()\nt = {{thresh}}\nyhat = (phat >= t).astype(int)\nTP = int(((yhat==1)&(y==1)).sum()); TN = int(((yhat==0)&(y==0)).sum())\nFP = int(((yhat==1)&(y==0)).sum()); FN = int(((yhat==0)&(y==1)).sum())\nacc = (TP+TN)/n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nbars = plt.bar(['TP','TN','FP','FN'], [TP,TN,FP,FN],\n               color=['#da9100','#af983f','#6f6b57','#c9c6ac'])\nfor b,v in zip(bars,[TP,TN,FP,FN]):\n    plt.text(b.get_x()+b.get_width()/2, v+4, str(v), ha='center', fontsize=9)\nplt.ylabel('count')\nplt.title('threshold=%.2f   accuracy=%.3f   (FP=%d, FN=%d)' % (t, acc, FP, FN))"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"A dataset is 95% zeros. A model that predicts 0 for everyone reports 95% accuracy. What does that reveal?","choices":["The model is excellent","Accuracy alone is misleading under class imbalance; check FN/TPR and AUC","Accuracy should always be replaced by pseudo-R²","The threshold must have been set above 0.95"],"answer":1,"hint":"How many true positives did that model catch?","explain":"It catches zero positives despite 95% accuracy, so accuracy is uninformative under imbalance — threshold-free measures like the ROC/AUC and the true-positive rate expose the failure."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"An ROC AUC of 0.5 means:","choices":["The model classifies everything perfectly","The model ranks positives above negatives no better than random chance","Exactly half the predictions are correct","The threshold is set to 0.5"],"answer":1,"hint":"AUC is the probability a random positive outscores a random negative.","explain":"AUC = 0.5 is the diagonal of the ROC plot — the model's probability ranking is no better than a coin flip, regardless of the chosen threshold."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check","prompt":"Raising the classification threshold from 0.5 to 0.8 will generally:","choices":["Increase both false positives and false negatives","Decrease false positives but increase false negatives","Decrease both error types simultaneously","Leave the confusion matrix unchanged"],"answer":1,"hint":"A higher bar to predict '1' makes the model more cautious about positives.","explain":"Demanding p̂ ≥ 0.8 makes positive predictions rarer, so false positives fall while genuine positives get missed more often, raising false negatives — the classic precision/recall trade-off."}]}]},"gmm":{"id":"gmm","title":"Generalized Method of Moments (GMM)","modules":[{"id":"gmm-moments","title":"1 · Moment conditions","summary":"GMM turns 'what we know about the population' into equations the data must satisfy, then minimizes how far the sample violates them.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"From assumptions to equations","html":"<h2>The one big idea</h2><p>Almost every estimator you know secretly says: <em>there is some quantity in the population that should equal zero.</em> GMM makes that statement the centerpiece.</p><p>A <strong>moment condition</strong> is a population statement of the form</p><p class=\"katexish\">E[ g(data, θ) ] = 0</p><p>where θ is the parameter we want and <code>g</code> is a function we choose because we believe its expectation is zero <em>at the true θ</em>. The vector <code>g</code> is called the <strong>moment function</strong>.</p><h3>The simplest example: a mean</h3><p>Suppose we want the population mean μ of a variable x. The defining property of the mean is that deviations average to zero:</p><p class=\"katexish\">E[ x − μ ] = 0</p><p>Here θ = μ and g(x, μ) = x − μ. That single equation <em>is</em> the model.</p><h3>From population to sample</h3><p>We cannot compute E[·], but we can compute its sample analog — the average over our n data points. Define the <strong>sample moment</strong>:</p><p class=\"katexish\">ḡ(θ) = (1/n) Σᵢ g(xᵢ, θ)</p><p>The <strong>method of moments</strong> simply sets ḡ(θ) = 0 and solves. For the mean, (1/n)Σ(xᵢ − μ) = 0 gives μ̂ = x̄. We just re-derived the sample average from a moment condition.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> A moment condition is a promise: \"at the true parameter, this thing averages to zero.\" Estimation = find the θ that makes the <em>sample</em> version as close to zero as possible.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"When you have more equations than unknowns","html":"<h2>Just-identified vs over-identified</h2><p>Count things. Let θ have <strong>k</strong> parameters and let g produce <strong>m</strong> moment equations.</p><ul><li><strong>m = k (just-identified):</strong> as many equations as unknowns. You can usually solve ḡ(θ) = 0 exactly, hitting zero on the nose.</li><li><strong>m &gt; k (over-identified):</strong> more equations than unknowns. In general <em>no</em> θ sets all of ḡ to zero simultaneously — the equations disagree slightly because of sampling noise.</li><li><strong>m &lt; k (under-identified):</strong> too few equations; θ is not pinned down. Not usable.</li></ul><p>The over-identified case is where GMM earns its name. We can't make every moment zero, so instead we make them <em>collectively small</em>. We minimize a weighted sum of squared moments:</p><p class=\"katexish\">θ̂ = argmin<sub>θ</sub>  ḡ(θ)′ · W · ḡ(θ)</p><p>where <strong>W</strong> is a positive-definite <strong>weighting matrix</strong> that says how much each moment (and each combination of moments) matters. This scalar quantity is the <strong>GMM objective</strong>.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> When m = k the choice of W is irrelevant (the minimum is exactly zero). When m &gt; k, W matters — and choosing it well is the whole game in Module 4.</div><p>Notice this nests the method of moments: if you <em>can</em> drive ḡ to zero, then ḡ′Wḡ = 0 for any W, so the GMM solution coincides with the exact solution.</p>"},{"type":"code","title":"Hand-roll GMM for a mean","note":"Estimate μ from the single moment E[x−μ]=0 by literally minimizing the GMM objective with scipy. With one moment and one parameter (m=k=1) the objective is driven to zero and μ̂ equals the sample mean.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 400\nx = rng.normal(5.0, 2.0, n)   # true mean 5\n\n# moment function g(x, mu) = x - mu  ->  sample moment is its average\ndef gbar(mu):\n    return np.array([np.mean(x - mu)])\n\n# GMM objective  J(mu) = gbar' W gbar,  here W = 1 (scalar, m=1)\ndef objective(p):\n    g = gbar(p[0])\n    return float(g @ g)\n\nres = minimize(objective, x0=[0.0])\nmu_hat = res.x[0]\n\nprint(f'sample mean x̄        = {x.mean():.6f}')\nprint(f'GMM μ̂ (minimizer)    = {mu_hat:.6f}')\nprint(f'objective at optimum  = {objective([mu_hat]):.3e}  (≈ 0, just-identified)')\nprint(f'gbar at optimum       = {gbar(mu_hat)[0]:.3e}')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Watch the GMM objective J(μ)","note":"The objective ḡ(μ)′ḡ(μ) is a parabola in μ that bottoms out exactly at the sample mean. Drag the candidate μ and read off J. The orange marker is your guess; the dashed line is where J is minimized (the sample mean).","params":[{"name":"mu","label":"Candidate μ","min":0,"max":10,"step":0.1,"value":2}],"template":"import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 400\nx = rng.normal(5.0, 2.0, n)\ndef J(mu):\n    g = np.mean(x - mu)\n    return g*g\ngrid = np.linspace(0,10,200)\nvals = [J(m) for m in grid]\nmu_star = x.mean()\nmu = {{mu}}\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(grid, vals, color='#af983f', lw=2, label='J(μ)=ḡ(μ)²')\nplt.axvline(mu_star, color='#6f6b57', ls='--', lw=1.5, label=f'min at x̄={mu_star:.2f}')\nplt.scatter([mu],[J(mu)], color='#da9100', s=70, zorder=5, label='your μ')\nplt.xlabel('candidate μ'); plt.ylabel('GMM objective J')\nplt.legend(fontsize=8)\nplt.title(f'μ={mu:.2f}  →  J={J(mu):.4f}   (min J at μ={mu_star:.2f})')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: counting moments","prompt":"You have a model with 2 parameters and you write down 5 moment conditions. This model is:","choices":["under-identified, so GMM cannot be used","just-identified, so the weighting matrix is irrelevant","over-identified, so GMM minimizes a weighted sum of moments and W matters","exactly-identified only if the moments are independent"],"answer":2,"hint":"Compare m (number of moments) with k (number of parameters).","explain":"With m = 5 moments and k = 2 parameters, m > k: the model is over-identified. You generally can't zero out all moments at once, so GMM minimizes ḡ′Wḡ and the choice of W changes the estimate."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: what is a moment condition?","prompt":"The moment condition E[x − μ] = 0 is used to estimate μ. What does the sample analog (1/n)Σ(xᵢ − μ̂) = 0 give you?","choices":["the sample variance","the sample mean x̄","the median of x","an arbitrary value depending on W"],"answer":1,"hint":"Solve (1/n)Σ(xᵢ − μ̂) = 0 for μ̂.","explain":"Setting the sample moment to zero gives Σxᵢ = nμ̂, i.e. μ̂ = x̄. The method-of-moments estimator of the mean is just the sample average."}]},{"id":"gmm-iv","title":"2 · IV as GMM","summary":"Instrumental variables is GMM with the moment 'instruments are uncorrelated with the error.' We derive the IV/2SLS estimator from that single idea.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"The instrument moment condition","html":"<h2>When a regressor is endogenous</h2><p>In the model y = β₀ + β₁x + ε, OLS needs E[x·ε] = 0. If x is <em>endogenous</em> — correlated with ε through omitted variables, simultaneity, or measurement error — OLS is biased.</p><p>An <strong>instrument</strong> z is a variable that is (i) <em>relevant</em> — correlated with x — and (ii) <em>exogenous</em> — uncorrelated with the error. Exogeneity is exactly a moment condition:</p><p class=\"katexish\">E[ z · ε ] = E[ z · (y − β₀ − β₁x) ] = 0</p><p>Stacking the constant (its own instrument) and z, the moment function is</p><p class=\"katexish\">g(θ) = Z′ · (y − Xβ),   where each row is zᵢ·(yᵢ − xᵢ′β)</p><p>The sample moment is ḡ(β) = (1/n) Z′(y − Xβ). This says: <em>the residuals must be orthogonal to the instruments.</em></p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> IV is not a separate technique — it is GMM whose moment condition is \"instruments are orthogonal to the error.\" OLS is the special case where the instruments are the regressors themselves (Z = X).</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Solving the just-identified case","html":"<h2>One instrument per endogenous regressor</h2><p>Suppose the number of instruments equals the number of parameters (m = k) — for one endogenous x with one instrument z, plus a constant. Then we can set the sample moment exactly to zero:</p><p class=\"katexish\">Z′(y − Xβ̂) = 0   ⟹   Z′Xβ̂ = Z′y   ⟹   β̂ = (Z′X)⁻¹ Z′y</p><p>That is the classic <strong>IV estimator</strong>. It is the just-identified GMM estimator, and because the system hits zero exactly, the weighting matrix W plays no role here.</p><h3>Why it fixes endogeneity</h3><p>Substitute y = Xβ + ε:</p><p class=\"katexish\">β̂ = β + (Z′X)⁻¹ Z′ε</p><p>As n grows, (1/n)Z′ε → E[z·ε] = 0 by the moment condition, so β̂ → β. The instrument's exogeneity is precisely what kills the bias term. Contrast OLS, where the analogous term is (X′X)⁻¹X′ε and (1/n)X′ε → E[x·ε] ≠ 0 when x is endogenous.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Relevance matters too.</b> If z barely moves x, then Z′X ≈ 0 and (Z′X)⁻¹ explodes — a <em>weak instrument</em>. The estimator stays consistent but becomes wildly imprecise. Strength of instruments is the theme of the slider below.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Endogeneity bias, then the IV fix","note":"We build a textbook endogenous case: x and ε share a common shock, so OLS is biased upward. The instrument z drives x but is independent of ε. Hand-rolled IV via the moment Z′(y−Xβ)=0 recovers the true slope β₁=2.","code":"import numpy as np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\n\nz = rng.normal(0, 1, n)            # instrument: relevant + exogenous\nc = rng.normal(0, 1, n)            # common confounder\nx = 0.8*z + c + rng.normal(0,1,n)  # x depends on z AND on confounder c\neps = c + rng.normal(0, 1, n)      # error shares c  -> x is endogenous\ny = 1 + 2*x + eps                  # true beta1 = 2\n\nX = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x])\nZ = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), z])\n\nb_ols = np.linalg.solve(X.T@X, X.T@y)\nb_iv  = np.linalg.solve(Z.T@X, Z.T@y)   # (Z'X)^-1 Z'y = just-identified GMM\n\nprint(f'true beta1            = 2.000')\nprint(f'OLS  beta1 (biased)   = {b_ols[1]:.3f}')\nprint(f'IV   beta1 (GMM mom.) = {b_iv[1]:.3f}')\nprint(f'check moment Z′(y-Xb_iv) = {np.round(Z.T@(y - X@b_iv), 6)}  (≈ 0)')"},{"type":"code","title":"Same answer from statsmodels (the production tool)","note":"In practice you call statsmodels rather than hand-rolling. LinearIVGMM and IV2SLS give the same just-identified estimate as our moment solver. Note the positional API: LinearIVGMM(endog=y, exog=X, instrument=Z).","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import LinearIVGMM, IV2SLS\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 800\nz = rng.normal(0,1,n); c = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx = 0.8*z + c + rng.normal(0,1,n)\neps = c + rng.normal(0,1,n)\ny = 1 + 2*x + eps\nX = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x])\nZ = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), z])\n\ngmm = LinearIVGMM(y, X, Z).fit()\ntsls = IV2SLS(y, X, Z).fit()\nprint('LinearIVGMM params:', np.round(gmm.params, 4))\nprint('IV2SLS      params:', np.round(tsls.params, 4))\nprint(f'\\nGMM slope = {gmm.params[1]:.4f}   (true = 2.0)')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Instrument strength vs. precision","note":"Slide the relevance coefficient (how strongly z drives x). Strong instruments give a tight, accurate IV slope; weak ones (near 0) leave the estimate noisy and unreliable even though it stays roughly centered on 2. The estimated slope and its SE are in the title.","params":[{"name":"relevance","label":"Instrument strength (z→x)","min":0.05,"max":1.5,"step":0.05,"value":0.8}],"template":"import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import LinearIVGMM\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 600\nz = rng.normal(0,1,n); c = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx = {{relevance}}*z + c + rng.normal(0,1,n)\neps = c + rng.normal(0,1,n)\ny = 1 + 2*x + eps\nX = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x])\nZ = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), z])\nm = LinearIVGMM(y, X, Z).fit()\nb1, se1 = m.params[1], m.bse[1]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.scatter(z, x, s=10, color='#da9100', alpha=.4)\nzz = np.linspace(z.min(), z.max(), 50)\nplt.plot(zz, np.polyval(np.polyfit(z,x,1), zz), color='#6f6b57', lw=2)\nplt.xlabel('instrument z'); plt.ylabel('regressor x')\nplt.title(f'strength={{relevance}}  →  IV β1={b1:.2f}  SE={se1:.3f}')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: which moment defines IV?","prompt":"What moment condition does instrumental-variables estimation impose to identify β?","choices":["E[x · ε] = 0  (regressor orthogonal to error)","E[z · ε] = 0  (instrument orthogonal to error)","E[ε²] = σ²  (homoskedasticity)","E[z · x] = 0  (instrument orthogonal to regressor)"],"answer":1,"hint":"OLS assumes the first one and fails when x is endogenous. IV replaces x with a variable assumed exogenous.","explain":"IV's identifying moment is E[z·ε] = 0: the instrument is uncorrelated with the error. (We also need E[z·x] ≠ 0 — relevance — but that is the opposite of choice 4.)"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: just-identified weighting","prompt":"In the just-identified IV case (one instrument per endogenous regressor), changing the GMM weighting matrix W:","choices":["changes the estimate a lot","has no effect, because the sample moment is set exactly to zero","is required to make the estimator consistent","only matters if the errors are heteroskedastic"],"answer":1,"hint":"Think about the value of ḡ′Wḡ at the solution when ḡ can be driven to exactly 0.","explain":"When m = k, β̂ = (Z′X)⁻¹Z′y makes ḡ(β̂) = 0 exactly, so ḡ′Wḡ = 0 for ANY W. The weighting matrix only matters once you are over-identified."}]},{"id":"gmm-overid","title":"3 · Over-identification & the J-test","summary":"Extra instruments give extra moment conditions. They can't all be satisfied at once — and that leftover disagreement becomes a test of whether your instruments are valid.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"More instruments than you need","html":"<h2>The over-identified case</h2><p>Now suppose you have <strong>two</strong> instruments z₁, z₂ for a single endogenous x (plus a constant). You have m = 3 moment conditions but only k = 2 parameters. The system</p><p class=\"katexish\">Z′(y − Xβ) = 0</p><p>has more equations than unknowns. In a finite sample there is generally <em>no</em> β that satisfies all three exactly — the extra instrument provides an independent check on the others.</p><p>GMM resolves the conflict by minimizing the weighted objective ḡ(β)′Wḡ(β). The minimizer makes the moments collectively small but leaves a nonzero <em>residual</em> ḡ(β̂). That leftover is informative:</p><ul><li>If all instruments are <strong>valid</strong> (truly E[zⱼ·ε] = 0), the leftover is just sampling noise and should be <em>small</em>.</li><li>If some instrument is <strong>invalid</strong> (correlated with ε), the moments fundamentally disagree and the leftover is <em>large</em>.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Over-identification is a gift: the surplus moments let the data tell you whether your identifying assumptions hold. Just-identified models can't be tested this way — they always fit their own moments perfectly.</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"Hansen's J-statistic","html":"<h2>Turning leftover moments into a test</h2><p>Evaluate the GMM objective at the optimum, using the <em>efficient</em> weighting matrix W = Ŝ⁻¹ (the inverse of the moment covariance — more on Ŝ in Module 4). Scaled by n, this is <strong>Hansen's J-statistic</strong>:</p><p class=\"katexish\">J = n · ḡ(β̂)′ Ŝ⁻¹ ḡ(β̂)</p><p>Under the null hypothesis that <em>all</em> moment conditions are valid, J has a chi-squared distribution:</p><p class=\"katexish\">J ~ χ²(m − k)</p><p>The degrees of freedom equal the number of <strong>over-identifying restrictions</strong>: m moments minus k parameters. With 3 instruments (including the constant) and 2 parameters, df = 1 — one surplus moment, one testable restriction.</p><h3>Reading the result</h3><ul><li><strong>Small J, large p-value:</strong> fail to reject. The instruments are mutually consistent — no evidence of invalidity.</li><li><strong>Large J, small p-value:</strong> reject. At least one moment condition is violated — an instrument is likely endogenous (or the model is misspecified).</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Careful.</b> The J-test is a <em>joint</em> test. Rejection says \"something is wrong with the set\" — it does not tell you <em>which</em> instrument is the culprit. And it can only test the surplus restrictions; if df = 0 the test is empty.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"Compute the J-statistic by hand","note":"Here Z = [constant, z1, z2, z3] gives m = 4 moments and k = 2 parameters, so df = 2. We do one-step GMM with W = (Z′Z/n)⁻¹, form S from the residuals, re-optimize with Ŝ⁻¹, then read J = n·ḡ′Ŝ⁻¹ḡ. All instruments are valid, so J is small and we do NOT reject.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\nfrom scipy import stats\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 600\n\nZc = rng.normal(0,1,(n,3))             # 3 candidate instruments\nconf = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx = Zc @ np.array([0.7,0.5,0.4]) + conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)\neps = conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)         # all z's independent of eps -> valid\ny = 1 + 2*x + eps\n\nX = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x])\nZ = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), Zc])  # m = 4 moments, k = 2 params -> df = 2\n\ndef gbar(b):\n    e = y - X@b\n    return (Z * e[:,None]).mean(0)\ndef obj(b, W):\n    g = gbar(b); return float(g @ W @ g)\n\nb0 = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)[0]\nW1 = np.linalg.inv((Z.T@Z)/n)\nb1 = minimize(obj, b0, args=(W1,)).x        # step 1\n\ne = y - X@b1\nS = (Z * e[:,None]).T @ (Z * e[:,None]) / n # moment covariance\nW2 = np.linalg.inv(S)\nb2 = minimize(obj, b1, args=(W2,)).x        # step 2 (efficient)\n\nJ = n * obj(b2, W2)\ndf = Z.shape[1] - X.shape[1]\npval = stats.chi2.sf(J, df)\nprint(f'GMM slope     = {b2[1]:.3f}  (true 2.0)')\nprint(f'J-statistic   = {J:.3f}   df = {df}')\nprint(f'p-value       = {pval:.3f}  ->  {\"REJECT\" if pval<0.05 else \"do NOT reject\"} validity')"},{"type":"code","title":"statsmodels reports jval for you","note":"LinearIVGMM exposes results.jval directly. We compare a clean setup (valid instruments) with a contaminated one where z2 is deliberately correlated with the error. Watch jval and the p-value jump when an instrument goes bad.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import LinearIVGMM\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ndef run(bad):\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n    n = 600\n    z1 = rng.normal(0,1,n); z2 = rng.normal(0,1,n); z3 = rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    conf = rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    x = 0.7*z1 + 0.5*z2 + 0.4*z3 + conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    eps = conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    if bad:\n        eps = eps + 1.2*z2          # z2 now correlated with error -> INVALID\n    y = 1 + 2*x + eps\n    X = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x])\n    Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), z1, z2, z3])\n    m = LinearIVGMM(y, X, Z).fit()\n    df = Z.shape[1] - X.shape[1]\n    p = stats.chi2.sf(m.jval, df)\n    return m.params[1], m.jval, df, p\n\nfor label, bad in [('valid instruments', False), ('z2 INVALID', True)]:\n    b1, J, df, p = run(bad)\n    print(f'{label:18s}  slope={b1:5.2f}  jval={J:7.2f}  df={df}  p={p:.4f}  -> {\"REJECT\" if p<0.05 else \"ok\"}')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Push an instrument out of validity","note":"The 'contamination' slider injects a correlation between instrument z2 and the error ε. At 0 the instruments are valid and J stays small (high p). Crank it up and the J-statistic explodes, the p-value collapses, and the test rejects. The J and p-value are in the title.","params":[{"name":"contam","label":"z2 ↔ error contamination","min":0,"max":1.5,"step":0.05,"value":0}],"template":"import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import LinearIVGMM\nfrom scipy import stats\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 600\nz1 = rng.normal(0,1,n); z2 = rng.normal(0,1,n); z3 = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nconf = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx = 0.7*z1 + 0.5*z2 + 0.4*z3 + conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)\neps = conf + rng.normal(0,1,n) + {{contam}}*z2\ny = 1 + 2*x + eps\nX = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x])\nZ = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), z1, z2, z3])\nm = LinearIVGMM(y, X, Z).fit()\ndf = Z.shape[1] - X.shape[1]\nJ = m.jval; p = stats.chi2.sf(J, df)\nxx = np.linspace(0, max(J*1.2, 12), 200)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.plot(xx, stats.chi2.pdf(xx, df), color='#c9c6ac', lw=2, label=f'χ²(df={df})')\nplt.axvline(J, color='#da9100', lw=2.5, label=f'J={J:.1f}')\ncrit = stats.chi2.ppf(0.95, df)\nplt.axvline(crit, color='#6f6b57', ls='--', lw=1.5, label=f'5% crit={crit:.1f}')\nplt.xlabel('J'); plt.legend(fontsize=8)\nplt.title(f'contam={{contam}}  →  J={J:.1f}  p={p:.3f}  ({\"REJECT\" if p<0.05 else \"ok\"})')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: degrees of freedom","prompt":"You estimate a model with 4 instruments (including the constant) and 2 parameters. The J-statistic has how many degrees of freedom?","choices":["4","2","6","1"],"answer":1,"hint":"df = number of over-identifying restrictions = m − k.","explain":"df = m − k = 4 − 2 = 2. There are two surplus moment conditions, so J ~ χ²(2) under the null that all instruments are valid."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: interpreting a small p-value","prompt":"Your J-test returns p = 0.002. What is the right conclusion?","choices":["All instruments are confirmed valid","Reject the null: at least one moment condition appears violated, so some instrument is likely invalid","The model is just-identified","The slope estimate must be biased upward"],"answer":1,"hint":"A small p-value means the over-identifying restrictions are rejected.","explain":"p = 0.002 rejects the joint null that all moments hold. Something in the instrument set is invalid (or the model is misspecified) — though the test does not tell you which instrument."}]},{"id":"gmm-efficient","title":"4 · Efficient (two-step) GMM","summary":"Among all weighting matrices, the inverse of the moment covariance gives the smallest variance. Two-step GMM estimates it from a first pass and re-optimizes.","stages":[{"type":"read","title":"The optimal weighting matrix","html":"<h2>Which W is best?</h2><p>When over-identified, different weighting matrices W give different (all consistent) estimates — but with different <em>precision</em>. Hansen's theorem pins down the best one. Let S be the covariance matrix of the (scaled) sample moments:</p><p class=\"katexish\">S = Var( √n · ḡ(θ) ) = E[ g·g′ ]   (at the true θ, when E[g]=0)</p><p>The <strong>efficient</strong> (minimum-variance) GMM estimator uses</p><p class=\"katexish\">W* = S⁻¹</p><p>Intuitively, S⁻¹ <em>downweights noisy moments and upweights precise ones</em>, and accounts for correlation between moments. Any other W yields a larger asymptotic variance. With W = S⁻¹, the estimator's variance simplifies to (G′S⁻¹G)⁻¹/n, where G is the Jacobian of the moments.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Key idea.</b> Efficiency is about the <em>weighting</em>, not the moments. Same moment conditions, smarter W = S⁻¹ → tighter standard errors. This is also the W that makes the J-statistic χ²-distributed (Module 3).</div>"},{"type":"read","title":"The chicken-and-egg, solved in two steps","html":"<h2>Why two steps?</h2><p>There's a circularity: S depends on the residuals, which depend on θ̂, which depends on W = S⁻¹. The standard fix is <strong>two-step (feasible) GMM</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Step 1.</strong> Pick a simple, consistent-but-inefficient W — commonly W = (Z′Z/n)⁻¹ or the identity. Minimize ḡ′Wḡ to get a preliminary θ̂₍₁₎. It's not efficient, but it's consistent — good enough to estimate S.</li><li><strong>Step 2.</strong> Use the step-1 residuals ê = y − Xθ̂₍₁₎ to form Ŝ = (1/n)Σ êᵢ² zᵢzᵢ′, set W = Ŝ⁻¹, and minimize again to get the efficient θ̂₍₂₎.</li></ul><p>You can iterate further (re-estimate S from step-2 residuals, repeat) until convergence — <strong>iterated GMM</strong> — but two steps already deliver the asymptotic efficiency gain.</p><p>Notice Ŝ uses each observation's squared residual: it is automatically <strong>heteroskedasticity-robust</strong>. That's a built-in bonus of efficient GMM — the standard errors don't assume constant error variance.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>In statsmodels.</b> <code>LinearIVGMM(...).fit()</code> performs two-step efficient GMM by default; the resulting <code>bse</code> are the efficient, robust standard errors and <code>jval</code> is the corresponding J-statistic.</div>"},{"type":"code","title":"One-step vs two-step: the efficiency gain","note":"A small Monte Carlo: for each simulated dataset, estimate the slope with the inefficient W=(Z′Z/n)⁻¹ (one-step) and with the efficient W=Ŝ⁻¹ (two-step). Deliberate heteroskedasticity makes the gap visible — the two-step standard deviation of estimates is smaller.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n\ndef gbar(b, X, Z, y):\n    e = y - X@b\n    return (Z * e[:,None]).mean(0)\ndef obj(b, W, X, Z, y):\n    g = gbar(b, X, Z, y); return float(g @ W @ g)\n\nb_one, b_two = [], []\nfor rep in range(150):\n    n = 400\n    Zc = rng.normal(0,1,(n,3)); conf = rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    x = Zc @ np.array([0.7,0.5,0.4]) + conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    # heteroskedastic error: spread grows with |z1|\n    scale = 0.5 + np.abs(Zc[:,0])\n    eps = conf + scale*rng.normal(0,1,n)\n    y = 1 + 2*x + eps\n    X = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x]); Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), Zc])\n    b0 = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None)[0]\n    W1 = np.linalg.inv((Z.T@Z)/n)\n    b1 = minimize(obj, b0, args=(W1,X,Z,y)).x\n    e = y - X@b1\n    S = (Z*e[:,None]).T @ (Z*e[:,None]) / n\n    b2 = minimize(obj, b1, args=(np.linalg.inv(S),X,Z,y)).x\n    b_one.append(b1[1]); b_two.append(b2[1])\n\nb_one = np.array(b_one); b_two = np.array(b_two)\nprint(f'one-step  slope: mean={b_one.mean():.3f}  sd={b_one.std():.4f}')\nprint(f'two-step  slope: mean={b_two.mean():.3f}  sd={b_two.std():.4f}  (smaller sd = more efficient)')\nprint(f'efficiency gain: sd reduced by {100*(1-b_two.std()/b_one.std()):.1f}%')"},{"type":"interactive","title":"Number of (valid) instruments → efficiency","note":"Adding valid, relevant instruments adds moment conditions and tightens efficient GMM. Slide the count of instruments used; the efficient two-step slope SE in the title shrinks as you add information. (All instruments here are valid, so consistency is preserved throughout.)","params":[{"name":"ninstr","label":"# instruments used","min":1,"max":5,"step":1,"value":1}],"template":"import numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom statsmodels.sandbox.regression.gmm import LinearIVGMM\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nn = 700\nk = int({{ninstr}})\ncoef = np.array([0.6,0.55,0.5,0.45,0.4])\nZc = rng.normal(0,1,(n,5))\nconf = rng.normal(0,1,n)\nx = Zc @ coef + conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)\neps = conf + rng.normal(0,1,n)\ny = 1 + 2*x + eps\nX = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x])\nZ = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), Zc[:, :k]])   # use first k instruments\nm = LinearIVGMM(y, X, Z).fit()\nb1, se1 = m.params[1], m.bse[1]\nplt.figure(figsize=(6,3.7))\nplt.bar(['β1 estimate'], [b1], yerr=[1.96*se1], color='#da9100',\n        ecolor='#6f6b57', capsize=8, width=0.5)\nplt.axhline(2.0, color='#af983f', ls='--', lw=1.5, label='true β1=2')\nplt.ylim(1.4, 2.6); plt.legend(fontsize=8)\nplt.title(f'{k} instrument(s)  →  efficient β1={b1:.3f}  SE={se1:.3f}')"},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: the efficient weighting matrix","prompt":"In efficient GMM, the optimal weighting matrix W* equals:","choices":["the identity matrix","(Z′Z)⁻¹, the first-step choice","S⁻¹, the inverse of the moment covariance matrix","the Jacobian G of the moment function"],"answer":2,"hint":"It downweights noisy moments and upweights precise ones — that's an inverse-variance idea.","explain":"Hansen's result: W* = S⁻¹ where S = Var(√n·ḡ). This inverse-variance weighting yields the minimum-variance (efficient) GMM estimator and makes the J-statistic χ²."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: why two steps?","prompt":"Two-step GMM is needed because:","choices":["the first step is biased and the second removes the bias","the efficient W = S⁻¹ depends on residuals, so we first get a consistent estimate to compute S, then re-optimize","scipy cannot minimize the objective in one pass","the J-test requires exactly two estimations"],"answer":1,"hint":"Both steps are consistent; only the variance differs. The issue is that S is unknown.","explain":"The efficient weight S⁻¹ depends on the unknown θ through the residuals. Step 1 gives a consistent (if inefficient) θ̂ to estimate S; step 2 plugs in Ŝ⁻¹ for the efficiency gain. Both steps are consistent — step 2 is just more precise."},{"type":"quiz","title":"Check: a hidden bonus","prompt":"Because the efficient weighting matrix is built from Ŝ = (1/n)Σ êᵢ²zᵢzᵢ′, two-step GMM standard errors are automatically:","choices":["biased toward zero","robust to heteroskedasticity","identical to OLS standard errors","invalid unless errors are normal"],"answer":1,"hint":"Each observation contributes its own squared residual êᵢ² — no constant-variance assumption is imposed.","explain":"Ŝ uses individual squared residuals, so it does not assume a constant error variance. Efficient two-step GMM therefore delivers heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors for free."}]}]}});
